window.D426_PODCAST = {"voices": {"A": "Alex (Piper lessac)", "B": "Sam (Piper ryan)"}, "episodes": {"blueprint": {"title": "Exam blueprint", "file": "audio/blueprint.mp3?v=12da4b95", "dur": 85.28, "segs": [{"t": 0.0, "spk": "B", "text": "Welcome to the D426 study podcast. I'm Sam."}, {"t": 4.03, "spk": "A", "text": "And I'm Alex."}, {"t": 5.6, "spk": "A", "text": "This episode is the exam blueprint: what's on the D426 objective assessment, and where to spend your time."}, {"t": 12.61, "spk": "B", "text": "The assessment is split into three competencies."}, {"t": 15.81, "spk": "A", "text": "Competency 1: Explains Database Attributes and SQL Commands - 28% of assessment. From zyBook Lessons 1 & 2."}, {"t": 24.43, "spk": "B", "text": "That covers DB basics, Relational model, SQL & data types, Core statements, Keys & constraints."}, {"t": 32.32, "spk": "A", "text": "Competency 2: Determines SQL Queries - 30% of assessment. From zyBook Lessons 3."}, {"t": 38.58, "spk": "B", "text": "That covers Writing queries, Joins & views."}, {"t": 41.78, "spk": "A", "text": "Competency 3: Defines Primary Keys - 42% of assessment. From zyBook Lessons 4 & 5."}, {"t": 48.9, "spk": "B", "text": "That covers ER modeling, Keys & normal forms, Physical design."}, {"t": 53.74, "spk": "A", "text": "Competency 3 is the biggest chunk at 42%, so it gets the most study time. Primary keys run through all of it: choosing them, candidate keys, normal forms, and how each kind of table gets its key."}, {"t": 66.49, "spk": "B", "text": "So the study order is biggest first: Competency 3, then Competency 2, then Competency 1."}, {"t": 73.45, "spk": "A", "text": "When you're done, take the exam simulation on the website. It's 30 questions split 8, 9, 13, the same proportions as the real exam."}, {"t": 82.8, "spk": "B", "text": "That's the blueprint."}, {"t": 83.89, "spk": "A", "text": "You've got this."}]}, "c3": {"title": "Competency 3: Defines Primary Keys", "file": "audio/c3.mp3?v=3d3e5fba", "dur": 702.56, "segs": [{"t": 0.0, "spk": "B", "text": "Welcome to the D426 study podcast. I'm Sam."}, {"t": 3.75, "spk": "A", "text": "And I'm Alex."}, {"t": 5.24, "spk": "A", "text": "This episode covers Competency 3: Defines Primary Keys - 42% of assessment."}, {"t": 10.68, "spk": "B", "text": "That's zyBook Lessons 4 & 5, in 3 modules: Design and ER modeling, Primary keys and normalization, Physical design."}, {"t": 20.25, "spk": "B", "text": "Module 8: Design and ER modeling.", "mod": "08-design-and-er-modeling"}, {"t": 23.22, "spk": "A", "text": "This one is part of Competency 3, Defines Primary Keys, worth 42% of the exam.", "mod": "08-design-and-er-modeling"}, {"t": 29.28, "spk": "B", "text": "Here's the short version.", "mod": "08-design-and-er-modeling"}, {"t": 30.75, "spk": "A", "text": "Three phases: Analysis → Logical design → Physical design.", "mod": "08-design-and-er-modeling"}, {"t": 35.18, "spk": "A", "text": "Eight steps: four in analysis, four in logical design. The last analysis step is supertype/subtype.", "mod": "08-design-and-er-modeling"}, {"t": 41.72, "spk": "A", "text": "Crow's foot: circle = 0, line = 1, crow's foot = many.", "mod": "08-design-and-er-modeling"}, {"t": 46.93, "spk": "A", "text": "Name a relationship by asking \"one or many?\" from both sides.", "mod": "08-design-and-er-modeling"}, {"t": 50.78, "spk": "B", "text": "Phases and steps.", "mod": "08-design-and-er-modeling"}, {"t": 52.28, "spk": "A", "text": "Analysis is also called conceptual design, ER modeling, or requirements definition.", "mod": "08-design-and-er-modeling"}, {"t": 58.02, "spk": "A", "text": "Physical design never changes results, only speed.", "mod": "08-design-and-er-modeling"}, {"t": 61.49, "spk": "B", "text": "ER building blocks.", "mod": "08-design-and-er-modeling"}, {"t": 63.2, "spk": "A", "text": "Entity. What it is: Person, place, product, concept, activity (rectangle). Becomes: Table.", "mod": "08-design-and-er-modeling"}, {"t": 70.54, "spk": "A", "text": "Relationship. What it is: A statement about two entities. Becomes: Foreign key.", "mod": "08-design-and-er-modeling"}, {"t": 76.39, "spk": "A", "text": "Attribute. What it is: A descriptive property. Becomes: Column.", "mod": "08-design-and-er-modeling"}, {"t": 81.0, "spk": "A", "text": "Reflexive relationship: an entity related to itself.", "mod": "08-design-and-er-modeling"}, {"t": 84.53, "spk": "A", "text": "Type vs instance: \"all employees\" is a type; \"Sam Snead\" is an instance.", "mod": "08-design-and-er-modeling"}, {"t": 89.57, "spk": "A", "text": "Intangible entity: in the model, but not tracked with data.", "mod": "08-design-and-er-modeling"}, {"t": 93.55, "spk": "B", "text": "Cardinality.", "mod": "08-design-and-er-modeling"}, {"t": 94.64, "spk": "A", "text": "Relationship maximum: the most instances that can relate (one or many).", "mod": "08-design-and-er-modeling"}, {"t": 99.52, "spk": "A", "text": "Relationship minimum: the fewest (zero = optional, one = required).", "mod": "08-design-and-er-modeling"}, {"t": 104.67, "spk": "A", "text": "Attribute notation: Name 1(1) = singular, required. Phone M(0) = plural, optional.", "mod": "08-design-and-er-modeling"}, {"t": 113.07, "spk": "A", "text": "Circle: Zero.", "mod": "08-design-and-er-modeling"}, {"t": 114.65, "spk": "A", "text": "Short line: One.", "mod": "08-design-and-er-modeling"}, {"t": 116.28, "spk": "A", "text": "Three lines: Many.", "mod": "08-design-and-er-modeling"}, {"t": 118.02, "spk": "B", "text": "Naming the relationship (exam favorite).", "mod": "08-design-and-er-modeling"}, {"t": 120.48, "spk": "A", "text": "The question's wording can trick you. Ask it from both sides, out loud:", "mod": "08-design-and-er-modeling"}, {"t": 125.09, "spk": "A", "text": "\"Can one A have many B?\"", "mod": "08-design-and-er-modeling"}, {"t": 126.88, "spk": "A", "text": "\"Can one B have many A?\"", "mod": "08-design-and-er-modeling"}, {"t": 128.85, "spk": "A", "text": "Yes and yes. Relationship: Many-to-many (M:M). Example: Employees ↔ projects, students ↔ classes, doctors ↔ patients.", "mod": "08-design-and-er-modeling"}, {"t": 140.46, "spk": "A", "text": "Yes and no. Relationship: One-to-many / many-to-one. Example: Many employees → one office building.", "mod": "08-design-and-er-modeling"}, {"t": 147.51, "spk": "A", "text": "No and no. Relationship: One-to-one (1:1). Example: Employee ↔ desk, when desks are never shared.", "mod": "08-design-and-er-modeling"}, {"t": 155.85, "spk": "A", "text": "\"Employee works on project\" sounds one-to-one. It isn't: projects have many employees, and employees can be on many projects.", "mod": "08-design-and-er-modeling"}, {"t": 163.67, "spk": "A", "text": "Degree = how many entity types are involved:", "mod": "08-design-and-er-modeling"}, {"t": 166.95, "spk": "A", "text": "Unary: one entity type related to itself (Employee supervises Employee).", "mod": "08-design-and-er-modeling"}, {"t": 171.89, "spk": "A", "text": "Binary: two entity types. The usual case.", "mod": "08-design-and-er-modeling"}, {"t": 175.27, "spk": "A", "text": "Ternary: three entity types.", "mod": "08-design-and-er-modeling"}, {"t": 177.52, "spk": "A", "text": "Modality is another name for the relationship minimum (optional vs required). Cardinality usually means the maximum.", "mod": "08-design-and-er-modeling"}, {"t": 184.35, "spk": "B", "text": "Strong, weak, super, sub.", "mod": "08-design-and-er-modeling"}, {"t": 187.1, "spk": "A", "text": "Strong entity: has its own identifying attribute.", "mod": "08-design-and-er-modeling"}, {"t": 190.46, "spk": "A", "text": "Weak entity: doesn't; depends on an identifying relationship to an identifying entity (an order line item depends on its order).", "mod": "08-design-and-er-modeling"}, {"t": 197.69, "spk": "A", "text": "On the diagram, a diamond sits next to the identifying entity. Its cardinality there is always 1(1).", "mod": "08-design-and-er-modeling"}, {"t": 204.59, "spk": "A", "text": "Subtype: a subset of a supertype (Manager in Employee). Drawn inside it, linked by an IsA relationship.", "mod": "08-design-and-er-modeling"}, {"t": 211.03, "spk": "A", "text": "Partition: a group of mutually exclusive subtypes (Car, Truck, Motorcycle in Vehicle).", "mod": "08-design-and-er-modeling"}, {"t": 216.73, "spk": "B", "text": "Exam traps. Watch out for these.", "mod": "08-design-and-er-modeling"}, {"t": 218.96, "spk": "A", "text": "\"Apply normal form\" is step 8, in logical design, not analysis.", "mod": "08-design-and-er-modeling"}, {"t": 223.71, "spk": "A", "text": "Weak ≠ intangible. Weak lacks an identifier; intangible isn't stored at all.", "mod": "08-design-and-er-modeling"}, {"t": 228.96, "spk": "B", "text": "Quick check. Try to answer before Alex does.", "mod": "08-design-and-er-modeling"}, {"t": 231.76, "spk": "B", "text": "Last of the four analysis steps?", "mod": "08-design-and-er-modeling"}, {"t": 236.54, "spk": "A", "text": "Create supertype and subtype entities.", "mod": "08-design-and-er-modeling"}, {"t": 239.6, "spk": "B", "text": "Circle + crow's foot means…?", "mod": "08-design-and-er-modeling"}, {"t": 244.27, "spk": "A", "text": "Zero or many.", "mod": "08-design-and-er-modeling"}, {"t": 245.91, "spk": "B", "text": "Relationship type becomes what in logical design?", "mod": "08-design-and-er-modeling"}, {"t": 251.45, "spk": "A", "text": "A foreign key.", "mod": "08-design-and-er-modeling"}, {"t": 253.15, "spk": "B", "text": "Students take many classes; classes have many students. Relationship?", "mod": "08-design-and-er-modeling"}, {"t": 260.07, "spk": "A", "text": "Many-to-many.", "mod": "08-design-and-er-modeling"}, {"t": 261.65, "spk": "B", "text": "That's module 8.", "mod": "08-design-and-er-modeling"}, {"t": 263.56, "spk": "B", "text": "Module 9: Primary keys and normalization.", "mod": "09-normalization"}, {"t": 266.68, "spk": "A", "text": "This one is part of Competency 3, Defines Primary Keys, worth 42% of the exam.", "mod": "09-normalization"}, {"t": 272.82, "spk": "B", "text": "Here's the short version.", "mod": "09-normalization"}, {"t": 274.26, "spk": "A", "text": "Good primary keys are stable, simple, meaningless.", "mod": "09-normalization"}, {"t": 278.02, "spk": "A", "text": "Every kind of table gets its key a specific way: weak = composite, subtype = copies the supertype's, many-to-many = both foreign keys.", "mod": "09-normalization"}, {"t": 286.86, "spk": "A", "text": "BCNF = whenever any column depends on B, B is unique. The gold standard.", "mod": "09-normalization"}, {"t": 292.49, "spk": "B", "text": "Turning the ER model into tables.", "mod": "09-normalization"}, {"t": 294.8, "spk": "A", "text": "Entity: Table with a primary key.", "mod": "09-normalization"}, {"t": 297.73, "spk": "A", "text": "One-to-many: Foreign key on the many side.", "mod": "09-normalization"}, {"t": 300.93, "spk": "A", "text": "One-to-one: Foreign key on one side (usually the table with fewer rows).", "mod": "09-normalization"}, {"t": 305.77, "spk": "A", "text": "Many-to-many: New table (an associative entity): two foreign keys = composite primary key.", "mod": "09-normalization"}, {"t": 312.11, "spk": "A", "text": "Plural attribute: New table with a foreign key back.", "mod": "09-normalization"}, {"t": 315.53, "spk": "A", "text": "Required attribute: NOT NULL.", "mod": "09-normalization"}, {"t": 318.04, "spk": "A", "text": "Unique attribute: UNIQUE.", "mod": "09-normalization"}, {"t": 320.08, "spk": "B", "text": "Choosing a primary key.", "mod": "09-normalization"}, {"t": 321.77, "spk": "A", "text": "Stable (doesn't change), simple (small, easy to type), meaningless (no descriptive info).", "mod": "09-normalization"}, {"t": 327.27, "spk": "A", "text": "Artificial key: designer-made, usually an auto-generated integer. Ticks all three boxes.", "mod": "09-normalization"}, {"t": 333.36, "spk": "A", "text": "Candidate key: unique and minimal. The designer picks one as the primary key.", "mod": "09-normalization"}, {"t": 338.35, "spk": "A", "text": "Non-key column: not part of any candidate key.", "mod": "09-normalization"}, {"t": 341.85, "spk": "A", "text": "Example: for Employee, an auto-generated EmployeeID beats name, email, or phone. Those can change or repeat.", "mod": "09-normalization"}, {"t": 349.33, "spk": "B", "text": "Where each table's primary key comes from.", "mod": "09-normalization"}, {"t": 351.68, "spk": "A", "text": "Strong entity → table: Its own identifying attribute, or an artificial key.", "mod": "09-normalization"}, {"t": 357.1, "spk": "A", "text": "Weak entity → weak table: Composite: a foreign key to the identifying table + another column.", "mod": "09-normalization"}, {"t": 363.18, "spk": "A", "text": "Subtype entity → subtype table: Same as the supertype's primary key, and it's also a foreign key to the supertype.", "mod": "09-normalization"}, {"t": 370.97, "spk": "A", "text": "Many-to-many → associative table: Composite of the two foreign keys.", "mod": "09-normalization"}, {"t": 375.82, "spk": "B", "text": "Dependence.", "mod": "09-normalization"}, {"t": 376.84, "spk": "A", "text": "Functional dependence: A depends on B if each B value maps to at most one A value (B → A).", "mod": "09-normalization"}, {"t": 382.71, "spk": "A", "text": "Redundancy: related values repeated in a table. Normal forms reduce it.", "mod": "09-normalization"}, {"t": 387.76, "spk": "A", "text": "Trivial dependency: A's columns are a subset of B's, so A always depends on B.", "mod": "09-normalization"}, {"t": 393.34, "spk": "B", "text": "The normal forms.", "mod": "09-normalization"}, {"t": 394.71, "spk": "A", "text": "1NF. Rule: One value per cell, has a primary key, non-key columns depend on it. Plain English: No lists in a cell.", "mod": "09-normalization"}, {"t": 403.52, "spk": "A", "text": "2NF. Rule: 1NF + non-key columns depend on the whole key. Plain English: Nothing hangs off half a composite key.", "mod": "09-normalization"}, {"t": 412.72, "spk": "A", "text": "3NF. Rule: Non-key A depends on B → B is unique. Plain English: No non-key → non-key dependence.", "mod": "09-normalization"}, {"t": 421.17, "spk": "A", "text": "BCNF. Rule: Any A depends on B → B is unique. Plain English: 3NF with \"non-key\" removed.", "mod": "09-normalization"}, {"t": 429.49, "spk": "A", "text": "Example: Employee(ID, Name, DeptCode, DeptName) breaks 3NF. DeptName depends on DeptCode, which repeats. Fix: move it to Department(DeptCode, DeptName).", "mod": "09-normalization"}, {"t": 443.1, "spk": "A", "text": "Normalization: split tables into higher normal forms. Last step of logical design.", "mod": "09-normalization"}, {"t": 448.99, "spk": "A", "text": "Denormalization: merge tables on purpose, adding redundancy (usually for faster reads).", "mod": "09-normalization"}, {"t": 454.62, "spk": "A", "text": "BCNF is ideal for tables with frequent inserts, updates, and deletes.", "mod": "09-normalization"}, {"t": 459.83, "spk": "B", "text": "Exam traps. Watch out for these.", "mod": "09-normalization"}, {"t": 462.13, "spk": "A", "text": "In 3NF/BCNF, B must be unique, not necessarily minimal, so B isn't always a candidate key.", "mod": "09-normalization"}, {"t": 469.77, "spk": "A", "text": "A candidate key must be minimal; \"unique\" alone isn't enough.", "mod": "09-normalization"}, {"t": 473.39, "spk": "A", "text": "A subtype table does not get a new key. It reuses the supertype's key, which doubles as a foreign key.", "mod": "09-normalization"}, {"t": 480.01, "spk": "A", "text": "A non-key column that depends on part of a composite key breaks 2NF.", "mod": "09-normalization"}, {"t": 484.95, "spk": "B", "text": "Quick check. Try to answer before Alex does.", "mod": "09-normalization"}, {"t": 487.69, "spk": "B", "text": "How do you implement a many-to-many relationship?", "mod": "09-normalization"}, {"t": 493.06, "spk": "A", "text": "A new table holding foreign keys to both tables.", "mod": "09-normalization"}, {"t": 496.56, "spk": "B", "text": "Three properties of a good primary key?", "mod": "09-normalization"}, {"t": 501.71, "spk": "A", "text": "Stable, simple, meaningless.", "mod": "09-normalization"}, {"t": 504.49, "spk": "B", "text": "Which form drops the word \"non-key\" from 3NF?", "mod": "09-normalization"}, {"t": 510.39, "spk": "A", "text": "BCNF.", "mod": "09-normalization"}, {"t": 512.19, "spk": "B", "text": "What's the primary key of a weak table? Of a subtype table?", "mod": "09-normalization"}, {"t": 518.34, "spk": "A", "text": "Weak: composite, a foreign key to the identifying table plus another column. Subtype: the supertype's primary key, which is also a foreign key.", "mod": "09-normalization"}, {"t": 527.47, "spk": "B", "text": "That's module 9.", "mod": "09-normalization"}, {"t": 529.48, "spk": "B", "text": "Module 10: Physical design.", "mod": "10-physical-design"}, {"t": 531.57, "spk": "A", "text": "This one is part of Competency 3, Defines Primary Keys, worth 42% of the exam.", "mod": "10-physical-design"}, {"t": 537.87, "spk": "B", "text": "Here's the short version.", "mod": "10-physical-design"}, {"t": 539.26, "spk": "A", "text": "Heap = fast inserts · sorted = fast ranges · hash = fast exact matches · cluster = fast joins.", "mod": "10-physical-design"}, {"t": 547.01, "spk": "A", "text": "High hit ratio → table scan. Low hit ratio → index scan.", "mod": "10-physical-design"}, {"t": 551.77, "spk": "A", "text": "Dense index = entry per row. Sparse = entry per block.", "mod": "10-physical-design"}, {"t": 556.09, "spk": "B", "text": "Table structures.", "mod": "10-physical-design"}, {"t": 557.38, "spk": "A", "text": "Heap. Rows stored: No order. Best for: Inserts, bulk loads. Weak at: Searching.", "mod": "10-physical-design"}, {"t": 563.65, "spk": "A", "text": "Sorted. Rows stored: By a sort column. Best for: Range queries, ORDER BY. Weak at: Inserts.", "mod": "10-physical-design"}, {"t": 571.12, "spk": "A", "text": "Hash. Rows stored: In buckets by hash key. Best for: Exact-match lookups. Weak at: Ranges.", "mod": "10-physical-design"}, {"t": 578.56, "spk": "A", "text": "Table cluster. Rows stored: Rows of 2+ tables interleaved. Best for: Joins on the cluster key. Weak at: Scanning one table.", "mod": "10-physical-design"}, {"t": 587.34, "spk": "A", "text": "Modulo hash: key ÷ number of buckets, remainder = bucket. Key 46 with 10 buckets → bucket 6.", "mod": "10-physical-design"}, {"t": 595.59, "spk": "B", "text": "Scans.", "mod": "10-physical-design"}, {"t": 596.46, "spk": "A", "text": "Table scan: reads table blocks directly, no index.", "mod": "10-physical-design"}, {"t": 600.43, "spk": "A", "text": "Index scan: reads the index to find which table blocks to read.", "mod": "10-physical-design"}, {"t": 605.02, "spk": "A", "text": "Hit ratio (filter factor, selectivity): % of rows the query selects.", "mod": "10-physical-design"}, {"t": 610.47, "spk": "A", "text": "Binary search: split the sorted index in half until you find it.", "mod": "10-physical-design"}, {"t": 614.47, "spk": "B", "text": "Index types.", "mod": "10-physical-design"}, {"t": 615.59, "spk": "A", "text": "Dense: Entry for every row.", "mod": "10-physical-design"}, {"t": 617.98, "spk": "A", "text": "Sparse: Entry for every block (table sorted on the column).", "mod": "10-physical-design"}, {"t": 621.59, "spk": "A", "text": "Hash: Entries in buckets.", "mod": "10-physical-design"}, {"t": 623.92, "spk": "A", "text": "Bitmap: Grid of 1s and 0s; great for few distinct values.", "mod": "10-physical-design"}, {"t": 628.59, "spk": "A", "text": "Logical: Stores primary key values, not block pointers.", "mod": "10-physical-design"}, {"t": 632.96, "spk": "A", "text": "Function: Indexes f(column), like UPPER(Name).", "mod": "10-physical-design"}, {"t": 636.78, "spk": "A", "text": "Multi-level: An index on an index.", "mod": "10-physical-design"}, {"t": 639.71, "spk": "A", "text": "There's a SQL example for this on the website. Look at it when you're back at a screen.", "mod": "10-physical-design"}, {"t": 644.75, "spk": "B", "text": "Storage.", "mod": "10-physical-design"}, {"t": 645.68, "spk": "A", "text": "Tablespace: maps one or more tables to a single file (CREATE TABLESPACE).", "mod": "10-physical-design"}, {"t": 650.44, "spk": "A", "text": "Partition: a subset of a table. Horizontal splits rows; vertical splits columns.", "mod": "10-physical-design"}, {"t": 656.15, "spk": "A", "text": "Storage engine: does the low-level reads/writes. MySQL default: InnoDB.", "mod": "10-physical-design"}, {"t": 661.69, "spk": "B", "text": "Exam traps. Watch out for these.", "mod": "10-physical-design"}, {"t": 663.99, "spk": "A", "text": "Indexes help low hit ratio queries. Selecting most rows? A table scan wins.", "mod": "10-physical-design"}, {"t": 669.7, "spk": "A", "text": "Physical design never changes results.", "mod": "10-physical-design"}, {"t": 672.25, "spk": "B", "text": "Quick check. Try to answer before Alex does.", "mod": "10-physical-design"}, {"t": 674.95, "spk": "B", "text": "Fastest structure for bulk loading?", "mod": "10-physical-design"}, {"t": 679.99, "spk": "A", "text": "Heap table.", "mod": "10-physical-design"}, {"t": 681.56, "spk": "B", "text": "A query returns 90% of rows. Table scan or index scan?", "mod": "10-physical-design"}, {"t": 688.69, "spk": "A", "text": "Table scan.", "mod": "10-physical-design"}, {"t": 690.35, "spk": "B", "text": "Which object maps tables to a single file?", "mod": "10-physical-design"}, {"t": 695.74, "spk": "A", "text": "Tablespace.", "mod": "10-physical-design"}, {"t": 697.39, "spk": "B", "text": "That's module 10.", "mod": "10-physical-design"}, {"t": 699.33, "spk": "B", "text": "That's all of Competency 3."}, {"t": 701.22, "spk": "A", "text": "You've got this."}]}, "c2": {"title": "Competency 2: Determines SQL Queries", "file": "audio/c2.mp3?v=54e3ea40", "dur": 326.6, "segs": [{"t": 0.0, "spk": "B", "text": "Welcome to the D426 study podcast. I'm Sam."}, {"t": 3.81, "spk": "A", "text": "And I'm Alex."}, {"t": 5.39, "spk": "A", "text": "This episode covers Competency 2: Determines SQL Queries - 30% of assessment."}, {"t": 11.31, "spk": "B", "text": "That's zyBook Lesson 3, in 2 modules: Writing queries, Joins, subqueries, and views."}, {"t": 18.1, "spk": "B", "text": "Module 6: Writing queries.", "mod": "06-writing-queries"}, {"t": 20.15, "spk": "A", "text": "This one is part of Competency 2, Determines SQL Queries, worth 30% of the exam.", "mod": "06-writing-queries"}, {"t": 26.76, "spk": "B", "text": "Here's the short version.", "mod": "06-writing-queries"}, {"t": 28.23, "spk": "A", "text": "Clause order: SELECT → FROM → WHERE → GROUP BY → HAVING → ORDER BY.", "mod": "06-writing-queries"}, {"t": 33.94, "spk": "A", "text": "WHERE filters rows; HAVING filters groups.", "mod": "06-writing-queries"}, {"t": 37.09, "spk": "A", "text": "LIKE: % = any number of characters, _ = exactly one.", "mod": "06-writing-queries"}, {"t": 42.58, "spk": "B", "text": "The whole thing in one query.", "mod": "06-writing-queries"}, {"t": 44.48, "spk": "A", "text": "There's a SQL example for this on the website. Look at it when you're back at a screen.", "mod": "06-writing-queries"}, {"t": 49.55, "spk": "A", "text": "Mnemonic: Sweaty Feet Will Give Horrible Odors.", "mod": "06-writing-queries"}, {"t": 52.69, "spk": "B", "text": "Filtering.", "mod": "06-writing-queries"}, {"t": 53.58, "spk": "A", "text": "BETWEEN. Meaning: Inclusive range. Example: Age BETWEEN 18 AND 30 = >= 18 AND <= 30.", "mod": "06-writing-queries"}, {"t": 61.88, "spk": "A", "text": "IN. Meaning: Any value in a list. Example: State IN ('PA', 'NJ').", "mod": "06-writing-queries"}, {"t": 67.43, "spk": "A", "text": "LIKE %. Meaning: Any number of characters (even zero). Example: 'L%t' → Lt, Lot, Lift.", "mod": "06-writing-queries"}, {"t": 76.44, "spk": "A", "text": "LIKE _. Meaning: Exactly one character. Example: 'L_t' → Lot, Lit (not Lt, Loot).", "mod": "06-writing-queries"}, {"t": 84.95, "spk": "A", "text": "IS NULL. Meaning: Tests for NULL. Example: = NULL never matches.", "mod": "06-writing-queries"}, {"t": 90.5, "spk": "B", "text": "Sorting and naming.", "mod": "06-writing-queries"}, {"t": 91.96, "spk": "A", "text": "ORDER BY sorts ascending by default. Add DESC for descending.", "mod": "06-writing-queries"}, {"t": 95.89, "spk": "A", "text": "Alias: a temporary name with optional AS: Salary AS Pay.", "mod": "06-writing-queries"}, {"t": 100.02, "spk": "B", "text": "Functions.", "mod": "06-writing-queries"}, {"t": 101.0, "spk": "A", "text": "ABS(n): ABS(-5) → 5.", "mod": "06-writing-queries"}, {"t": 104.75, "spk": "A", "text": "LOWER(s): LOWER('MySQL') → 'mysql'.", "mod": "06-writing-queries"}, {"t": 108.3, "spk": "A", "text": "TRIM(s): TRIM(' test ') → 'test'.", "mod": "06-writing-queries"}, {"t": 111.05, "spk": "A", "text": "HOUR / MINUTE / SECOND: HOUR('22:11:45') → 22.", "mod": "06-writing-queries"}, {"t": 116.61, "spk": "A", "text": "UPPER(s): UPPER('sql') → 'SQL'.", "mod": "06-writing-queries"}, {"t": 119.84, "spk": "A", "text": "CONCAT(s1, s2, ...): CONCAT('Data', 'base') → 'Database'.", "mod": "06-writing-queries"}, {"t": 125.06, "spk": "A", "text": "SUBSTRING(s, pos, len): SUBSTRING('Database', 1, 4) → 'Data'.", "mod": "06-writing-queries"}, {"t": 131.37, "spk": "A", "text": "REPLACE(s, from, to): REPLACE('cats and dogs', 'and', 'or') → 'cats or dogs'.", "mod": "06-writing-queries"}, {"t": 137.16, "spk": "B", "text": "Aggregates.", "mod": "06-writing-queries"}, {"t": 138.16, "spk": "A", "text": "COUNT, MIN, MAX, SUM, AVG turn many rows into one value.", "mod": "06-writing-queries"}, {"t": 143.41, "spk": "A", "text": "They only see rows that pass WHERE.", "mod": "06-writing-queries"}, {"t": 145.74, "spk": "A", "text": "With GROUP BY, they compute once per group.", "mod": "06-writing-queries"}, {"t": 148.6, "spk": "A", "text": "They skip NULLs; COUNT(*) still counts every row.", "mod": "06-writing-queries"}, {"t": 151.91, "spk": "B", "text": "Exam traps. Watch out for these.", "mod": "06-writing-queries"}, {"t": 154.26, "spk": "A", "text": "Filtering on an aggregate (COUNT(*) > 5) needs HAVING, not WHERE.", "mod": "06-writing-queries"}, {"t": 158.65, "spk": "A", "text": "BETWEEN includes both ends.", "mod": "06-writing-queries"}, {"t": 160.78, "spk": "B", "text": "Quick check. Try to answer before Alex does.", "mod": "06-writing-queries"}, {"t": 163.51, "spk": "B", "text": "Which clause comes right after GROUP BY?", "mod": "06-writing-queries"}, {"t": 168.46, "spk": "A", "text": "HAVING.", "mod": "06-writing-queries"}, {"t": 169.73, "spk": "B", "text": "Does LIKE 'L_t' match \"Loot\"?", "mod": "06-writing-queries"}, {"t": 174.53, "spk": "A", "text": "No. _ is exactly one character.", "mod": "06-writing-queries"}, {"t": 177.62, "spk": "B", "text": "AVG skips which values?", "mod": "06-writing-queries"}, {"t": 182.35, "spk": "A", "text": "NULLs.", "mod": "06-writing-queries"}, {"t": 183.59, "spk": "B", "text": "That's module 6.", "mod": "06-writing-queries"}, {"t": 185.66, "spk": "B", "text": "Module 7: Joins, subqueries, and views.", "mod": "07-joins-subqueries-views"}, {"t": 189.32, "spk": "A", "text": "This one is part of Competency 2, Determines SQL Queries, worth 30% of the exam.", "mod": "07-joins-subqueries-views"}, {"t": 195.83, "spk": "B", "text": "Here's the short version.", "mod": "07-joins-subqueries-views"}, {"t": 197.22, "spk": "A", "text": "The join type decides what happens to unmatched rows.", "mod": "07-joins-subqueries-views"}, {"t": 200.5, "spk": "A", "text": "LEFT keeps all left rows; RIGHT keeps all right rows; FULL keeps everything.", "mod": "07-joins-subqueries-views"}, {"t": 205.73, "spk": "A", "text": "A materialized view stores its data and must be refreshed.", "mod": "07-joins-subqueries-views"}, {"t": 209.58, "spk": "B", "text": "Which rows survive?.", "mod": "07-joins-subqueries-views"}, {"t": 210.84, "spk": "A", "text": "INNER. Keeps: Only matches. Unmatched side shows: Dropped.", "mod": "07-joins-subqueries-views"}, {"t": 215.54, "spk": "A", "text": "LEFT. Keeps: All left + matching right. Unmatched side shows: NULL in right columns.", "mod": "07-joins-subqueries-views"}, {"t": 221.64, "spk": "A", "text": "RIGHT. Keeps: All right + matching left. Unmatched side shows: NULL in left columns.", "mod": "07-joins-subqueries-views"}, {"t": 227.76, "spk": "A", "text": "FULL. Keeps: Everything from both. Unmatched side shows: NULL wherever no match.", "mod": "07-joins-subqueries-views"}, {"t": 233.41, "spk": "A", "text": "CROSS. Keeps: Every combination; no ON clause. Unmatched side shows: n/a.", "mod": "07-joins-subqueries-views"}, {"t": 239.71, "spk": "A", "text": "There's a SQL example for this on the website. Look at it when you're back at a screen.", "mod": "07-joins-subqueries-views"}, {"t": 244.85, "spk": "B", "text": "More join words.", "mod": "07-joins-subqueries-views"}, {"t": 246.32, "spk": "A", "text": "Outer join = LEFT, RIGHT, or FULL (anything that keeps unmatched rows).", "mod": "07-joins-subqueries-views"}, {"t": 250.66, "spk": "A", "text": "Equijoin uses =. Non-equijoin uses <, >, and so on.", "mod": "07-joins-subqueries-views"}, {"t": 254.91, "spk": "A", "text": "Self-join: a table joined to itself (employee → manager). Use aliases.", "mod": "07-joins-subqueries-views"}, {"t": 260.32, "spk": "A", "text": "UNION stacks two SELECT results into one. MySQL has no FULL JOIN, so you do LEFT JOIN UNION RIGHT JOIN.", "mod": "07-joins-subqueries-views"}, {"t": 267.67, "spk": "B", "text": "Subqueries.", "mod": "07-joins-subqueries-views"}, {"t": 268.84, "spk": "A", "text": "A query inside a query. Also called a nested or inner query.", "mod": "07-joins-subqueries-views"}, {"t": 273.23, "spk": "B", "text": "Views.", "mod": "07-joins-subqueries-views"}, {"t": 273.9, "spk": "A", "text": "View: a table defined by a SELECT. The tables it reads are its base tables.", "mod": "07-joins-subqueries-views"}, {"t": 278.71, "spk": "A", "text": "Materialized view: stores its data; must be refreshed when base tables change.", "mod": "07-joins-subqueries-views"}, {"t": 284.36, "spk": "A", "text": "WITH CHECK OPTION: rejects inserts/updates through the view that don't match the view's WHERE.", "mod": "07-joins-subqueries-views"}, {"t": 289.73, "spk": "B", "text": "Exam traps. Watch out for these.", "mod": "07-joins-subqueries-views"}, {"t": 291.95, "spk": "A", "text": "CROSS JOIN has no ON clause.", "mod": "07-joins-subqueries-views"}, {"t": 294.22, "spk": "A", "text": "\"Outer join\" never means INNER.", "mod": "07-joins-subqueries-views"}, {"t": 296.41, "spk": "B", "text": "Quick check. Try to answer before Alex does.", "mod": "07-joins-subqueries-views"}, {"t": 299.18, "spk": "B", "text": "You need every employee, even ones with no department. Which join (Employee on the left)?", "mod": "07-joins-subqueries-views"}, {"t": 306.36, "spk": "A", "text": "LEFT JOIN.", "mod": "07-joins-subqueries-views"}, {"t": 307.94, "spk": "B", "text": "What do you call a join of a table to itself?", "mod": "07-joins-subqueries-views"}, {"t": 313.17, "spk": "A", "text": "Self-join.", "mod": "07-joins-subqueries-views"}, {"t": 314.7, "spk": "B", "text": "Which view type must be refreshed?", "mod": "07-joins-subqueries-views"}, {"t": 319.39, "spk": "A", "text": "Materialized view.", "mod": "07-joins-subqueries-views"}, {"t": 321.45, "spk": "B", "text": "That's module 7.", "mod": "07-joins-subqueries-views"}, {"t": 323.42, "spk": "B", "text": "That's all of Competency 2."}, {"t": 325.24, "spk": "A", "text": "You've got this."}]}, "c1": {"title": "Competency 1: Explains Database Attributes and SQL Commands", "file": "audio/c1.mp3?v=11d8fca1", "dur": 828.5, "segs": [{"t": 0.0, "spk": "B", "text": "Welcome to the D426 study podcast. I'm Sam."}, {"t": 3.71, "spk": "A", "text": "And I'm Alex."}, {"t": 5.37, "spk": "A", "text": "This episode covers Competency 1: Explains Database Attributes and SQL Commands - 28% of assessment."}, {"t": 12.98, "spk": "B", "text": "That's zyBook Lessons 1 & 2, in 5 modules: Database basics, Relational model, SQL language and data types, Core statements, Keys and constraints."}, {"t": 24.69, "spk": "B", "text": "Module 1: Database basics.", "mod": "01-database-basics"}, {"t": 27.27, "spk": "A", "text": "This one is part of Competency 1, Explains Database Attributes and SQL Commands, worth 28% of the exam.", "mod": "01-database-basics"}, {"t": 35.21, "spk": "B", "text": "Here's the short version.", "mod": "01-database-basics"}, {"t": 36.62, "spk": "A", "text": "Query processor plans the query. Storage manager reads and writes files. Transaction manager keeps things consistent.", "mod": "01-database-basics"}, {"t": 43.72, "spk": "A", "text": "The DBA secures the database against unauthorized users.", "mod": "01-database-basics"}, {"t": 47.57, "spk": "A", "text": "Authorization limits users to specific tables, columns, or rows.", "mod": "01-database-basics"}, {"t": 52.08, "spk": "B", "text": "How a query moves through the system.", "mod": "01-database-basics"}, {"t": 54.39, "spk": "A", "text": "Query processor: Interprets the query, builds a plan, runs query optimization, returns results.", "mod": "01-database-basics"}, {"t": 60.93, "spk": "A", "text": "Storage manager: Turns the plan into low-level file-system commands; uses indexes to find data fast.", "mod": "01-database-basics"}, {"t": 67.65, "spk": "A", "text": "Transaction manager: Prevents conflicts between concurrent transactions; restores consistency after a failure.", "mod": "01-database-basics"}, {"t": 74.74, "spk": "A", "text": "Log: Records every insert, update, and delete for recovery.", "mod": "01-database-basics"}, {"t": 79.22, "spk": "A", "text": "Catalog (data dictionary): Metadata about tables, columns, indexes.", "mod": "01-database-basics"}, {"t": 84.35, "spk": "B", "text": "People and rules.", "mod": "01-database-basics"}, {"t": 85.73, "spk": "A", "text": "Database application: software that helps business users work with a database.", "mod": "01-database-basics"}, {"t": 90.77, "spk": "A", "text": "Database administrator (DBA): secures the system and enforces access and availability procedures.", "mod": "01-database-basics"}, {"t": 97.13, "spk": "A", "text": "Other roles: database designer (structure), database programmer (writes code that uses SQL), database user (consumes the data).", "mod": "01-database-basics"}, {"t": 105.66, "spk": "A", "text": "Rules: data must stay consistent with structural and business rules.", "mod": "01-database-basics"}, {"t": 110.04, "spk": "A", "text": "API: lets Python, Java, and other languages run SQL.", "mod": "01-database-basics"}, {"t": 114.92, "spk": "A", "text": "MySQL is relational and open source. MongoDB is NoSQL and open source.", "mod": "01-database-basics"}, {"t": 120.87, "spk": "B", "text": "Exam traps. Watch out for these.", "mod": "01-database-basics"}, {"t": 123.22, "spk": "A", "text": "Query optimization belongs to the query processor, not the storage manager.", "mod": "01-database-basics"}, {"t": 127.8, "spk": "A", "text": "Recovery after a crash is the transaction manager.", "mod": "01-database-basics"}, {"t": 131.08, "spk": "B", "text": "Quick check. Try to answer before Alex does.", "mod": "01-database-basics"}, {"t": 133.84, "spk": "B", "text": "Which component translates the plan into file-system commands?", "mod": "01-database-basics"}, {"t": 140.05, "spk": "A", "text": "Storage manager.", "mod": "01-database-basics"}, {"t": 141.68, "spk": "B", "text": "Who secures the database against unauthorized users?", "mod": "01-database-basics"}, {"t": 147.68, "spk": "A", "text": "The database administrator (DBA).", "mod": "01-database-basics"}, {"t": 150.54, "spk": "B", "text": "What prevents two concurrent transactions from colliding?", "mod": "01-database-basics"}, {"t": 156.34, "spk": "A", "text": "The transaction manager.", "mod": "01-database-basics"}, {"t": 158.56, "spk": "B", "text": "That's module 1.", "mod": "01-database-basics"}, {"t": 160.58, "spk": "B", "text": "Module 2: Relational model.", "mod": "02-relational-model"}, {"t": 162.87, "spk": "A", "text": "This one is part of Competency 1, Explains Database Attributes and SQL Commands, worth 28% of the exam.", "mod": "02-relational-model"}, {"t": 171.13, "spk": "B", "text": "Here's the short version.", "mod": "02-relational-model"}, {"t": 172.55, "spk": "A", "text": "A table = a name + a fixed sequence of columns + a varying set of rows.", "mod": "02-relational-model"}, {"t": 177.54, "spk": "A", "text": "Rows have no order (it's a set). Tuples do have order.", "mod": "02-relational-model"}, {"t": 180.8, "spk": "A", "text": "Data independence: how data is stored never changes query results.", "mod": "02-relational-model"}, {"t": 185.58, "spk": "B", "text": "The building blocks.", "mod": "02-relational-model"}, {"t": 187.01, "spk": "A", "text": "A table needs at least one column, but can have zero rows (an empty table).", "mod": "02-relational-model"}, {"t": 191.82, "spk": "A", "text": "Column: has a name and a data type.", "mod": "02-relational-model"}, {"t": 194.41, "spk": "A", "text": "Row: an unnamed tuple of values, one per column.", "mod": "02-relational-model"}, {"t": 197.98, "spk": "A", "text": "Cell: one column of one row.", "mod": "02-relational-model"}, {"t": 200.41, "spk": "A", "text": "Tuple: ordered, so (a, b, c) ≠ (c, b, a).", "mod": "02-relational-model"}, {"t": 205.0, "spk": "A", "text": "Data type: a named set of values that column values come from.", "mod": "02-relational-model"}, {"t": 209.4, "spk": "B", "text": "Same thing, three names.", "mod": "02-relational-model"}, {"t": 211.19, "spk": "A", "text": "Relation. SQL: Table. Old file term: File.", "mod": "02-relational-model"}, {"t": 215.75, "spk": "A", "text": "Tuple. SQL: Row. Old file term: Record.", "mod": "02-relational-model"}, {"t": 220.05, "spk": "A", "text": "Attribute. SQL: Column. Old file term: Field.", "mod": "02-relational-model"}, {"t": 224.53, "spk": "B", "text": "Relational algebra, the three to know.", "mod": "02-relational-model"}, {"t": 226.9, "spk": "A", "text": "Select. Does: Picks rows. SQL feel: WHERE.", "mod": "02-relational-model"}, {"t": 231.38, "spk": "A", "text": "Project. Does: Picks columns. SQL feel: SELECT Col1, Col2.", "mod": "02-relational-model"}, {"t": 237.79, "spk": "A", "text": "Product. Does: Every row × every row (Table1 × Table2). SQL feel: CROSS JOIN.", "mod": "02-relational-model"}, {"t": 245.07, "spk": "A", "text": "Trick: Project = Pillars (columns stand up).", "mod": "02-relational-model"}, {"t": 248.52, "spk": "B", "text": "Two kinds of rules.", "mod": "02-relational-model"}, {"t": 249.96, "spk": "A", "text": "Relational (structural) rules apply to every relational database: unique primary key, unique column names, no duplicate rows.", "mod": "02-relational-model"}, {"t": 258.42, "spk": "A", "text": "Business rules come from business policy and apply to one database: \"a course in a registration must exist in the catalog.\"", "mod": "02-relational-model"}, {"t": 265.01, "spk": "B", "text": "Exam traps. Watch out for these.", "mod": "02-relational-model"}, {"t": 267.25, "spk": "A", "text": "\"Attribute\" = column, not row.", "mod": "02-relational-model"}, {"t": 270.19, "spk": "A", "text": "A table with no rows is still valid.", "mod": "02-relational-model"}, {"t": 272.72, "spk": "B", "text": "Quick check. Try to answer before Alex does.", "mod": "02-relational-model"}, {"t": 275.46, "spk": "B", "text": "A row is also called a…?", "mod": "02-relational-model"}, {"t": 280.28, "spk": "A", "text": "Tuple (or record).", "mod": "02-relational-model"}, {"t": 281.94, "spk": "B", "text": "Why do rows have no inherent order?", "mod": "02-relational-model"}, {"t": 286.71, "spk": "A", "text": "A table is a set of rows, and sets are unordered.", "mod": "02-relational-model"}, {"t": 290.06, "spk": "B", "text": "What principle says physical storage changes never change results?", "mod": "02-relational-model"}, {"t": 296.8, "spk": "A", "text": "Data independence.", "mod": "02-relational-model"}, {"t": 298.86, "spk": "B", "text": "That's module 2.", "mod": "02-relational-model"}, {"t": 300.81, "spk": "B", "text": "Module 3: SQL language and data types.", "mod": "03-sql-language-and-types"}, {"t": 304.23, "spk": "A", "text": "This one is part of Competency 1, Explains Database Attributes and SQL Commands, worth 28% of the exam.", "mod": "03-sql-language-and-types"}, {"t": 312.22, "spk": "B", "text": "Here's the short version.", "mod": "03-sql-language-and-types"}, {"t": 313.64, "spk": "A", "text": "Five sublanguages: DDL (structure), DQL (SELECT), DML (change data), DCL (access), DTL (transactions).", "mod": "03-sql-language-and-types"}, {"t": 322.68, "spk": "A", "text": "DECIMAL(M, D) = M total digits, D after the decimal.", "mod": "03-sql-language-and-types"}, {"t": 326.95, "spk": "A", "text": "Integer sizes: TINYINT 1 byte · SMALLINT 2 · MEDIUMINT 3 · INT 4 · BIGINT 8.", "mod": "03-sql-language-and-types"}, {"t": 333.2, "spk": "B", "text": "The five sublanguages.", "mod": "03-sql-language-and-types"}, {"t": 334.86, "spk": "A", "text": "DDL Data Definition. Job: Defines structure. Statements: CREATE, ALTER, DROP.", "mod": "03-sql-language-and-types"}, {"t": 341.83, "spk": "A", "text": "DQL Data Query. Job: Retrieves data. Statements: SELECT.", "mod": "03-sql-language-and-types"}, {"t": 347.14, "spk": "A", "text": "DML Data Manipulation. Job: Changes data. Statements: INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE.", "mod": "03-sql-language-and-types"}, {"t": 353.68, "spk": "A", "text": "DCL Data Control. Job: Controls access. Statements: GRANT, REVOKE.", "mod": "03-sql-language-and-types"}, {"t": 359.68, "spk": "A", "text": "DTL Data Transaction. Job: Manages transactions. Statements: COMMIT, ROLLBACK.", "mod": "03-sql-language-and-types"}, {"t": 366.54, "spk": "B", "text": "Parts of a statement.", "mod": "03-sql-language-and-types"}, {"t": 368.03, "spk": "A", "text": "Literal (explicit value): 'String', \"String\", 123, x'0fa2'.", "mod": "03-sql-language-and-types"}, {"t": 374.12, "spk": "A", "text": "Keyword (special meaning): SELECT, FROM, WHERE.", "mod": "03-sql-language-and-types"}, {"t": 377.74, "spk": "A", "text": "Identifier (object name): City, Name, Population.", "mod": "03-sql-language-and-types"}, {"t": 382.05, "spk": "A", "text": "Expression (evaluates to one value): Population > 1000000 OR Name = 'Tokyo'.", "mod": "03-sql-language-and-types"}, {"t": 388.26, "spk": "A", "text": "Comment (ignored): -- one line, /* many lines */.", "mod": "03-sql-language-and-types"}, {"t": 392.16, "spk": "B", "text": "Data types.", "mod": "03-sql-language-and-types"}, {"t": 393.25, "spk": "A", "text": "INT / INTEGER. Stores: Whole numbers, 4 bytes. Example: 34, -739448.", "mod": "03-sql-language-and-types"}, {"t": 402.24, "spk": "A", "text": "DECIMAL(M, D). Stores: M digits total, D after the point. Example: DECIMAL(5, 2) → 999.99 max.", "mod": "03-sql-language-and-types"}, {"t": 412.06, "spk": "A", "text": "VARCHAR(N). Stores: Text, 0 to N characters. Example: 'Seattle'.", "mod": "03-sql-language-and-types"}, {"t": 417.9, "spk": "A", "text": "CHAR(N). Stores: Text, fixed length N. Example: 'PA' in CHAR(2).", "mod": "03-sql-language-and-types"}, {"t": 423.63, "spk": "A", "text": "DATE. Stores: Year, month, day. Example: '2026-11-08'.", "mod": "03-sql-language-and-types"}, {"t": 430.49, "spk": "A", "text": "TINYINT. Bytes: 1. Signed: -128 to 127. Unsigned: 0 to 255.", "mod": "03-sql-language-and-types"}, {"t": 439.38, "spk": "A", "text": "SMALLINT. Bytes: 2. Signed: -32,768 to 32,767. Unsigned: 0 to 65,535.", "mod": "03-sql-language-and-types"}, {"t": 451.21, "spk": "A", "text": "MEDIUMINT. Bytes: 3. Signed: -8,388,608 to 8,388,607. Unsigned: 0 to 16,777,215.", "mod": "03-sql-language-and-types"}, {"t": 466.26, "spk": "A", "text": "INT. Bytes: 4. Signed: about ±2.1 billion. Unsigned: 0 to 4,294,967,295.", "mod": "03-sql-language-and-types"}, {"t": 477.92, "spk": "A", "text": "BIGINT. Bytes: 8. Signed: -2^63 to 2^63 - 1. Unsigned: 0 to 2^64 - 1.", "mod": "03-sql-language-and-types"}, {"t": 488.58, "spk": "B", "text": "Operators worth remembering.", "mod": "03-sql-language-and-types"}, {"t": 490.36, "spk": "A", "text": "% modulo = remainder: 5 % 2 → 1", "mod": "03-sql-language-and-types"}, {"t": 494.95, "spk": "A", "text": "^ = power in the course table: 5^2 → 25", "mod": "03-sql-language-and-types"}, {"t": 499.89, "spk": "A", "text": "'2019-08-13' > '2021-08-13' → FALSE (dates compare in order)", "mod": "03-sql-language-and-types"}, {"t": 508.45, "spk": "B", "text": "Exam traps. Watch out for these.", "mod": "03-sql-language-and-types"}, {"t": 510.63, "spk": "A", "text": "SELECT is DQL, not DML. (Some study sheets lump it into DML; the five-sublanguage model doesn't.)", "mod": "03-sql-language-and-types"}, {"t": 517.78, "spk": "A", "text": "DECIMAL(5, 2) is 5 total digits, not 5 before the decimal.", "mod": "03-sql-language-and-types"}, {"t": 521.99, "spk": "A", "text": "Real MySQL treats ^ as XOR, but answer with the course table.", "mod": "03-sql-language-and-types"}, {"t": 525.79, "spk": "B", "text": "Quick check. Try to answer before Alex does.", "mod": "03-sql-language-and-types"}, {"t": 528.56, "spk": "B", "text": "GRANT belongs to which sublanguage?", "mod": "03-sql-language-and-types"}, {"t": 533.34, "spk": "A", "text": "DCL.", "mod": "03-sql-language-and-types"}, {"t": 534.88, "spk": "B", "text": "How many bytes is SMALLINT?", "mod": "03-sql-language-and-types"}, {"t": 539.36, "spk": "A", "text": "2.", "mod": "03-sql-language-and-types"}, {"t": 540.48, "spk": "B", "text": "Largest value DECIMAL(5, 2) can hold?", "mod": "03-sql-language-and-types"}, {"t": 546.06, "spk": "A", "text": "999.99.", "mod": "03-sql-language-and-types"}, {"t": 549.16, "spk": "B", "text": "That's module 3.", "mod": "03-sql-language-and-types"}, {"t": 551.12, "spk": "B", "text": "Module 4: Core statements.", "mod": "04-core-statements"}, {"t": 553.57, "spk": "A", "text": "This one is part of Competency 1, Explains Database Attributes and SQL Commands, worth 28% of the exam.", "mod": "04-core-statements"}, {"t": 561.82, "spk": "B", "text": "Here's the short version.", "mod": "04-core-statements"}, {"t": 563.19, "spk": "A", "text": "DELETE = chosen rows · TRUNCATE = all rows · DROP = the whole table.", "mod": "04-core-statements"}, {"t": 568.71, "spk": "A", "text": "UPDATE or DELETE without WHERE changes every row.", "mod": "04-core-statements"}, {"t": 571.89, "spk": "A", "text": "MERGE copies from a source table into a target table.", "mod": "04-core-statements"}, {"t": 575.1, "spk": "B", "text": "What each one does.", "mod": "04-core-statements"}, {"t": 576.64, "spk": "A", "text": "CREATE TABLE. Does: Makes a table: name, columns, data types. Watch for: Constraints go here too.", "mod": "04-core-statements"}, {"t": 584.2, "spk": "A", "text": "DROP TABLE. Does: Deletes the table and its rows. Watch for: Structure is gone.", "mod": "04-core-statements"}, {"t": 589.99, "spk": "A", "text": "ALTER TABLE. Does: ADD, DROP, or CHANGE columns. Watch for: Also adds/drops constraints.", "mod": "04-core-statements"}, {"t": 597.38, "spk": "A", "text": "INSERT. Does: Adds rows. Watch for: Skip values for auto-increment keys.", "mod": "04-core-statements"}, {"t": 603.49, "spk": "A", "text": "UPDATE. Does: Changes rows with SET. Watch for: No WHERE = every row.", "mod": "04-core-statements"}, {"t": 609.41, "spk": "A", "text": "DELETE. Does: Removes rows. Watch for: No WHERE = every row.", "mod": "04-core-statements"}, {"t": 614.69, "spk": "A", "text": "TRUNCATE. Does: Removes all rows, keeps the table. Watch for: ≈ DELETE with no WHERE.", "mod": "04-core-statements"}, {"t": 620.91, "spk": "A", "text": "MERGE. Does: Source → target.", "mod": "04-core-statements"}, {"t": 623.85, "spk": "B", "text": "See them in action.", "mod": "04-core-statements"}, {"t": 625.11, "spk": "A", "text": "There's a SQL example for this on the website. Look at it when you're back at a screen.", "mod": "04-core-statements"}, {"t": 630.35, "spk": "A", "text": "A syntax error in the MySQL Command-Line Client returns an error code and description.", "mod": "04-core-statements"}, {"t": 635.71, "spk": "B", "text": "Exam traps. Watch out for these.", "mod": "04-core-statements"}, {"t": 637.91, "spk": "A", "text": "DROP removes the table itself; TRUNCATE leaves an empty table.", "mod": "04-core-statements"}, {"t": 641.8, "spk": "A", "text": "The INSERT above skips ID on purpose: it's auto-increment.", "mod": "04-core-statements"}, {"t": 645.71, "spk": "B", "text": "Quick check. Try to answer before Alex does.", "mod": "04-core-statements"}, {"t": 648.56, "spk": "B", "text": "UPDATE Employee SET Salary = 0; — what happens?", "mod": "04-core-statements"}, {"t": 654.66, "spk": "A", "text": "Every row's salary becomes 0. No WHERE clause.", "mod": "04-core-statements"}, {"t": 658.64, "spk": "B", "text": "Which statement removes all rows but keeps the table?", "mod": "04-core-statements"}, {"t": 664.35, "spk": "A", "text": "TRUNCATE.", "mod": "04-core-statements"}, {"t": 665.77, "spk": "B", "text": "Which clause does ALTER TABLE use to rename/modify a column?", "mod": "04-core-statements"}, {"t": 672.15, "spk": "A", "text": "CHANGE (alongside ADD and DROP).", "mod": "04-core-statements"}, {"t": 674.88, "spk": "B", "text": "That's module 4.", "mod": "04-core-statements"}, {"t": 676.86, "spk": "B", "text": "Module 5: Keys and constraints.", "mod": "05-keys-and-constraints"}, {"t": 679.43, "spk": "A", "text": "This one is part of Competency 1, Explains Database Attributes and SQL Commands, worth 28% of the exam.", "mod": "05-keys-and-constraints"}, {"t": 687.41, "spk": "B", "text": "Here's the short version.", "mod": "05-keys-and-constraints"}, {"t": 688.95, "spk": "A", "text": "Primary key identifies each row: unique and not NULL.", "mod": "05-keys-and-constraints"}, {"t": 692.96, "spk": "A", "text": "Foreign key points at a primary key: same data type, name can differ.", "mod": "05-keys-and-constraints"}, {"t": 697.38, "spk": "A", "text": "Four fix-ups for broken references: RESTRICT, SET NULL, SET DEFAULT, CASCADE.", "mod": "05-keys-and-constraints"}, {"t": 703.06, "spk": "B", "text": "Primary keys.", "mod": "05-keys-and-constraints"}, {"t": 704.31, "spk": "A", "text": "Simple = one column. Composite = several columns.", "mod": "05-keys-and-constraints"}, {"t": 708.13, "spk": "A", "text": "Auto-increment column: gets the next number automatically on insert.", "mod": "05-keys-and-constraints"}, {"t": 712.35, "spk": "A", "text": "Two classic insert mistakes:", "mod": "05-keys-and-constraints"}, {"t": 714.43, "spk": "A", "text": "Supplying a value for an auto-increment key.", "mod": "05-keys-and-constraints"}, {"t": 717.23, "spk": "A", "text": "Leaving out the value for a key that is not auto-increment.", "mod": "05-keys-and-constraints"}, {"t": 720.41, "spk": "B", "text": "Foreign keys and referential integrity.", "mod": "05-keys-and-constraints"}, {"t": 723.01, "spk": "A", "text": "Declared with FOREIGN KEY (...) REFERENCES Table(...).", "mod": "05-keys-and-constraints"}, {"t": 725.44, "spk": "A", "text": "Referential integrity: every foreign key value matches an existing primary key, or is NULL.", "mod": "05-keys-and-constraints"}, {"t": 731.53, "spk": "A", "text": "RESTRICT: Reject the change.", "mod": "05-keys-and-constraints"}, {"t": 733.99, "spk": "A", "text": "SET NULL: Set the broken foreign keys to NULL.", "mod": "05-keys-and-constraints"}, {"t": 737.18, "spk": "A", "text": "SET DEFAULT: Set them to the column's default value.", "mod": "05-keys-and-constraints"}, {"t": 740.49, "spk": "A", "text": "CASCADE: Carry the change through (ON DELETE CASCADE deletes the child rows).", "mod": "05-keys-and-constraints"}, {"t": 745.32, "spk": "A", "text": "There's a SQL example for this on the website. Look at it when you're back at a screen.", "mod": "05-keys-and-constraints"}, {"t": 750.48, "spk": "B", "text": "Constraints.", "mod": "05-keys-and-constraints"}, {"t": 751.49, "spk": "A", "text": "A constraint is a rule on allowed values. Break it and the statement is rejected.", "mod": "05-keys-and-constraints"}, {"t": 756.42, "spk": "A", "text": "NOT NULL: Can't be empty.", "mod": "05-keys-and-constraints"}, {"t": 758.87, "spk": "A", "text": "UNIQUE: No duplicates.", "mod": "05-keys-and-constraints"}, {"t": 761.04, "spk": "A", "text": "PRIMARY KEY: Unique + not NULL.", "mod": "05-keys-and-constraints"}, {"t": 764.09, "spk": "A", "text": "FOREIGN KEY: Must match a referenced key.", "mod": "05-keys-and-constraints"}, {"t": 767.05, "spk": "A", "text": "CHECK: Must pass a condition: CHECK (Salary > 0).", "mod": "05-keys-and-constraints"}, {"t": 771.16, "spk": "A", "text": "DEFAULT: Value used when none is given.", "mod": "05-keys-and-constraints"}, {"t": 774.06, "spk": "A", "text": "Column constraint: written right after one column's definition.", "mod": "05-keys-and-constraints"}, {"t": 778.05, "spk": "A", "text": "Table constraint: its own clause; can cover several columns, like PRIMARY KEY (A, B).", "mod": "05-keys-and-constraints"}, {"t": 784.15, "spk": "A", "text": "Change them later with ALTER TABLE ... ADD / DROP / CHANGE.", "mod": "05-keys-and-constraints"}, {"t": 787.28, "spk": "B", "text": "Exam traps. Watch out for these.", "mod": "05-keys-and-constraints"}, {"t": 789.52, "spk": "A", "text": "Foreign key and primary key names may differ; types must match.", "mod": "05-keys-and-constraints"}, {"t": 793.55, "spk": "A", "text": "CASCADE propagates. RESTRICT rejects. Don't swap them.", "mod": "05-keys-and-constraints"}, {"t": 797.31, "spk": "B", "text": "Quick check. Try to answer before Alex does.", "mod": "05-keys-and-constraints"}, {"t": 799.97, "spk": "B", "text": "Deleting a department should delete its employees too. Which action?", "mod": "05-keys-and-constraints"}, {"t": 806.26, "spk": "A", "text": "ON DELETE CASCADE.", "mod": "05-keys-and-constraints"}, {"t": 808.42, "spk": "B", "text": "Which constraint enforces \"Salary must be positive\"?", "mod": "05-keys-and-constraints"}, {"t": 814.03, "spk": "A", "text": "CHECK (Salary > 0).", "mod": "05-keys-and-constraints"}, {"t": 816.33, "spk": "B", "text": "Primary key made of two columns?", "mod": "05-keys-and-constraints"}, {"t": 821.1, "spk": "A", "text": "Composite primary key.", "mod": "05-keys-and-constraints"}, {"t": 823.16, "spk": "B", "text": "That's module 5.", "mod": "05-keys-and-constraints"}, {"t": 825.22, "spk": "B", "text": "That's all of Competency 1."}, {"t": 827.16, "spk": "A", "text": "You've got this."}]}, "cram": {"title": "Cram sheet, rapid fire", "file": "audio/cram.mp3?v=dfc8c51b", "dur": 203.27, "segs": [{"t": 0.0, "spk": "B", "text": "Welcome to the D426 study podcast. I'm Sam."}, {"t": 3.74, "spk": "A", "text": "And I'm Alex."}, {"t": 5.29, "spk": "A", "text": "Rapid fire cram sheet. Ten facts, then the pairs people mix up."}, {"t": 9.81, "spk": "B", "text": "First, the blueprint."}, {"t": 11.37, "spk": "A", "text": "Competency 1: Explains Database Attributes and SQL Commands - 28% of assessment."}, {"t": 17.74, "spk": "A", "text": "Competency 2: Determines SQL Queries - 30% of assessment."}, {"t": 22.51, "spk": "A", "text": "Competency 3: Defines Primary Keys - 42% of assessment."}, {"t": 27.29, "spk": "B", "text": "Fact 1: Clause order."}, {"t": 29.05, "spk": "A", "text": "SELECT → FROM → WHERE → GROUP BY → HAVING → ORDER BY (Sweaty Feet Will Give Horrible Odors)"}, {"t": 35.91, "spk": "B", "text": "Fact 2: WHERE vs HAVING."}, {"t": 38.27, "spk": "A", "text": "WHERE filters rows before grouping; HAVING filters groups after GROUP BY"}, {"t": 43.19, "spk": "B", "text": "Fact 3: Design phases."}, {"t": 45.09, "spk": "A", "text": "Analysis (ER model) → Logical (tables, keys, normalization) → Physical (indexes, storage)"}, {"t": 52.78, "spk": "B", "text": "Fact 4: Data independence."}, {"t": 55.22, "spk": "A", "text": "Physical design changes speed, never query results"}, {"t": 59.09, "spk": "B", "text": "Fact 5: Foreign key."}, {"t": 60.78, "spk": "A", "text": "Refers to a primary key; data types must match, names can differ"}, {"t": 65.95, "spk": "B", "text": "Fact 6: FK actions."}, {"t": 68.14, "spk": "A", "text": "RESTRICT rejects · SET NULL · SET DEFAULT · CASCADE propagates"}, {"t": 73.14, "spk": "B", "text": "Fact 7: Joins."}, {"t": 74.88, "spk": "A", "text": "INNER = matches · LEFT = all left · RIGHT = all right · FULL = all · CROSS = every combo"}, {"t": 82.29, "spk": "B", "text": "Fact 8: 3NF vs BCNF."}, {"t": 85.47, "spk": "A", "text": "3NF: non-key columns depend only on unique columns. BCNF: every column does"}, {"t": 92.89, "spk": "B", "text": "Fact 9: Sublanguages."}, {"t": 94.9, "spk": "A", "text": "DDL structure · DQL SELECT · DML change data · DCL access · DTL transactions"}, {"t": 102.32, "spk": "B", "text": "Fact 10: Danger zone."}, {"t": 104.24, "spk": "A", "text": "UPDATE or DELETE with no WHERE hits every row"}, {"t": 107.56, "spk": "B", "text": "Now, don't mix these up."}, {"t": 109.29, "spk": "B", "text": "WHERE vs HAVING."}, {"t": 110.52, "spk": "A", "text": "WHERE filters rows before grouping; HAVING filters groups after"}, {"t": 114.77, "spk": "B", "text": "DELETE vs TRUNCATE vs DROP."}, {"t": 117.12, "spk": "A", "text": "Some rows · all rows · the whole table"}, {"t": 119.85, "spk": "B", "text": "Logical vs physical design."}, {"t": 122.0, "spk": "A", "text": "Tables, columns, keys · indexes, structures, partitions"}, {"t": 127.24, "spk": "B", "text": "Candidate vs primary vs artificial key."}, {"t": 130.17, "spk": "A", "text": "Unique + minimal · the one chosen · designer-made integer"}, {"t": 134.19, "spk": "B", "text": "3NF vs BCNF."}, {"t": 136.6, "spk": "A", "text": "Rule covers non-key columns · rule covers every column"}, {"t": 140.51, "spk": "B", "text": "Normalization vs denormalization."}, {"t": 142.92, "spk": "A", "text": "Split tables to remove redundancy · merge to add it back"}, {"t": 147.12, "spk": "B", "text": "Dense vs sparse index."}, {"t": 149.17, "spk": "A", "text": "Entry per row · entry per block"}, {"t": 151.81, "spk": "B", "text": "Table scan vs index scan."}, {"t": 153.95, "spk": "A", "text": "High hit ratio · low hit ratio"}, {"t": 156.67, "spk": "B", "text": "View vs materialized view."}, {"t": 158.63, "spk": "A", "text": "Runs query on use · stores data, needs refresh"}, {"t": 162.87, "spk": "B", "text": "Strong vs weak entity."}, {"t": 164.76, "spk": "A", "text": "Own identifying attribute · depends on identifying relationship"}, {"t": 169.25, "spk": "B", "text": "% vs _ in LIKE."}, {"t": 171.76, "spk": "A", "text": "Any number of characters · exactly one"}, {"t": 175.06, "spk": "B", "text": "Heap vs sorted vs hash."}, {"t": 177.1, "spk": "A", "text": "Fast inserts · fast ranges · fast exact matches"}, {"t": 181.46, "spk": "B", "text": "Unary vs binary vs ternary."}, {"t": 183.74, "spk": "A", "text": "One entity type · two · three"}, {"t": 186.21, "spk": "B", "text": "Cardinality vs modality."}, {"t": 188.21, "spk": "A", "text": "Maximum (one or many) · minimum (zero or one)"}, {"t": 191.9, "spk": "B", "text": "Weak table vs subtype table key."}, {"t": 194.12, "spk": "A", "text": "FK to identifying table + another column · same key as the supertype (also an FK)"}, {"t": 200.02, "spk": "B", "text": "That's the cram sheet. Go get it."}, {"t": 201.85, "spk": "A", "text": "You've got this."}]}, "quiz-c3": {"title": "Quiz show: Competency 3", "file": "audio/quiz-c3.mp3?v=4897fa01", "dur": 839.63, "segs": [{"t": 0.0, "spk": "B", "text": "Welcome to the D426 study podcast. I'm Sam."}, {"t": 3.72, "spk": "A", "text": "And I'm Alex."}, {"t": 5.33, "spk": "A", "text": "Quiz show: every practice question for Competency 3, Defines Primary Keys. That's 36 questions."}, {"t": 12.56, "spk": "B", "text": "I'll read the question and the choices, you answer in your head, then Alex explains."}, {"t": 17.62, "spk": "B", "text": "Question 1. Which phase captures requirements as entities, relationships, and attributes, independent of any database system?"}, {"t": 25.01, "spk": "B", "text": "A: Analysis. B: Logical design. C: Physical design. D: Normalization."}, {"t": 33.52, "spk": "A", "text": "The answer is A: Analysis."}, {"t": 36.11, "spk": "A", "text": "Also called conceptual design, ER modeling, or requirements definition."}, {"t": 41.84, "spk": "B", "text": "Question 2. Which phase adds indexes and specifies how tables are organized on storage?"}, {"t": 47.49, "spk": "B", "text": "A: Analysis. B: Logical design. C: Physical design. D: Requirements definition."}, {"t": 56.38, "spk": "A", "text": "The answer is C: Physical design."}, {"t": 59.11, "spk": "A", "text": "Physical design affects speed, never results."}, {"t": 62.67, "spk": "B", "text": "Question 3. What is the last of the four analysis steps?"}, {"t": 66.23, "spk": "B", "text": "A: Determine cardinality. B: Apply normal form. C: Distinguish strong and weak entities. D: Create supertype and subtype entities."}, {"t": 77.68, "spk": "A", "text": "The answer is D: Create supertype and subtype entities."}, {"t": 81.69, "spk": "A", "text": "1 discover, 2 cardinality, 3 strong/weak, 4 supertype/subtype. Normal form is step 8."}, {"t": 88.94, "spk": "B", "text": "Question 4. In crow's foot notation, three short lines mean:"}, {"t": 93.07, "spk": "B", "text": "A: Zero. B: One. C: Many. D: Optional."}, {"t": 99.57, "spk": "A", "text": "The answer is C: Many."}, {"t": 101.89, "spk": "A", "text": "Circle = zero, line = one, crow's foot = many."}, {"t": 106.61, "spk": "B", "text": "Question 5. A weak entity is one that:"}, {"t": 109.25, "spk": "B", "text": "A: Has no attributes. B: Has no identifying attribute and depends on an identifying relationship. C: Has a composite primary key. D: Is never stored in the database."}, {"t": 122.56, "spk": "A", "text": "The answer is B: Has no identifying attribute and depends on an identifying relationship."}, {"t": 127.77, "spk": "A", "text": "Not stored at all = intangible entity."}, {"t": 131.44, "spk": "B", "text": "Question 6. A partition of a supertype entity is:"}, {"t": 135.23, "spk": "B", "text": "A: A horizontal split of table rows. B: A group of mutually exclusive subtype entities. C: A weak entity. D: A reflexive relationship."}, {"t": 146.84, "spk": "A", "text": "The answer is B: A group of mutually exclusive subtype entities."}, {"t": 151.09, "spk": "A", "text": "Car, Truck, Motorcycle partition Vehicle."}, {"t": 154.71, "spk": "B", "text": "Question 7. Employees can work on many projects, and each project has many employees. What is the relationship?"}, {"t": 161.41, "spk": "B", "text": "A: One-to-one. B: Many-to-one. C: Many-to-many. D: One-to-many from project to employee only."}, {"t": 170.14, "spk": "A", "text": "The answer is C: Many-to-many."}, {"t": 172.56, "spk": "A", "text": "Read it from both sides: many on each side means many-to-many."}, {"t": 177.26, "spk": "B", "text": "Question 8. Many employees are assigned to one office building, and each employee has only one building. Employee to building is:"}, {"t": 184.52, "spk": "B", "text": "A: Many-to-one. B: One-to-one. C: Many-to-many. D: Unary."}, {"t": 191.86, "spk": "A", "text": "The answer is A: Many-to-one."}, {"t": 193.98, "spk": "A", "text": "Many employees, one building each: many-to-one."}, {"t": 197.99, "spk": "B", "text": "Question 9. Each employee is assigned exactly one desk, and desks are never shared. The relationship is:"}, {"t": 204.58, "spk": "B", "text": "A: Many-to-many. B: One-to-one. C: Many-to-one. D: Ternary."}, {"t": 211.44, "spk": "A", "text": "The answer is B: One-to-one."}, {"t": 213.63, "spk": "A", "text": "One-to-one needs a rule that blocks sharing on both sides."}, {"t": 217.55, "spk": "B", "text": "Question 10. A relationship where Employee supervises Employee is:"}, {"t": 221.97, "spk": "B", "text": "A: Binary. B: Ternary. C: Unary. D: Associative."}, {"t": 229.17, "spk": "A", "text": "The answer is C: Unary."}, {"t": 231.44, "spk": "A", "text": "One entity type related to itself: unary (reflexive)."}, {"t": 235.73, "spk": "B", "text": "Question 11. Relationship minimum is also called:"}, {"t": 239.06, "spk": "B", "text": "A: Cardinality maximum. B: Modality. C: Degree. D: Selectivity."}, {"t": 247.18, "spk": "A", "text": "The answer is B: Modality."}, {"t": 249.61, "spk": "A", "text": "Modality = minimum (optional vs required)."}, {"t": 253.56, "spk": "B", "text": "Question 12. In an ER diagram, where does the diamond for a weak entity's identifying relationship go?"}, {"t": 259.76, "spk": "B", "text": "A: Next to the weak entity. B: Next to the identifying entity. C: On every relationship. D: Inside the attribute list."}, {"t": 270.73, "spk": "A", "text": "The answer is B: Next to the identifying entity."}, {"t": 274.06, "spk": "A", "text": "It sits by the identifying entity, whose cardinality is always 1(1)."}, {"t": 279.87, "spk": "B", "text": "Question 13. In logical design, an entity type becomes a:"}, {"t": 284.32, "spk": "B", "text": "A: Column. B: Table. C: Foreign key. D: Index."}, {"t": 291.31, "spk": "A", "text": "The answer is B: Table."}, {"t": 293.44, "spk": "A", "text": "Entity → table, relationship → foreign key, attribute → column."}, {"t": 299.63, "spk": "B", "text": "Question 14. Good primary keys should be:"}, {"t": 302.69, "spk": "B", "text": "A: Descriptive, long, and changeable. B: Stable, simple, and meaningless. C: Composite and descriptive. D: Text-based for readability."}, {"t": 314.44, "spk": "A", "text": "The answer is B: Stable, simple, and meaningless."}, {"t": 318.27, "spk": "A", "text": "Artificial keys meet all three."}, {"t": 321.12, "spk": "B", "text": "Question 15. A candidate key is a column or group of columns that is:"}, {"t": 325.95, "spk": "B", "text": "A: Unique and minimal. B: Unique but not necessarily minimal. C: Always auto-increment. D: A foreign key."}, {"t": 335.76, "spk": "A", "text": "The answer is A: Unique and minimal."}, {"t": 338.33, "spk": "A", "text": "The designer picks one candidate key as the primary key."}, {"t": 342.37, "spk": "B", "text": "Question 16. 3NF: whenever a non-key column A depends on column B, then:"}, {"t": 348.06, "spk": "B", "text": "A: B is a foreign key. B: B is unique. C: A is unique. D: B is a non-key column."}, {"t": 357.55, "spk": "A", "text": "The answer is B: B is unique."}, {"t": 359.93, "spk": "A", "text": "B must be unique, but not necessarily minimal."}, {"t": 363.71, "spk": "B", "text": "Question 17. How does BCNF differ from 3NF?"}, {"t": 367.64, "spk": "B", "text": "A: BCNF allows more redundancy. B: BCNF's rule applies to every column, not just non-key columns. C: BCNF only applies to composite keys. D: No difference."}, {"t": 382.18, "spk": "A", "text": "The answer is B: BCNF's rule applies to every column, not just non-key columns."}, {"t": 388.13, "spk": "A", "text": "BCNF is the gold standard; ideal for frequent inserts, updates, deletes."}, {"t": 394.26, "spk": "B", "text": "Question 18. Denormalization means:"}, {"t": 397.11, "spk": "B", "text": "A: Decomposing tables into higher normal forms. B: Intentionally adding redundancy by merging tables. C: Removing all foreign keys. D: Deleting duplicate rows."}, {"t": 410.47, "spk": "A", "text": "The answer is B: Intentionally adding redundancy by merging tables."}, {"t": 414.83, "spk": "A", "text": "Normalization splits; denormalization merges."}, {"t": 418.68, "spk": "B", "text": "Question 19. How is a many-to-many relationship implemented?"}, {"t": 422.5, "spk": "B", "text": "A: A foreign key on either table. B: A new table with foreign keys to both entities. C: Merging the two tables. D: A CHECK constraint."}, {"t": 433.68, "spk": "A", "text": "The answer is B: A new table with foreign keys to both entities."}, {"t": 437.62, "spk": "A", "text": "The two foreign keys form a composite primary key."}, {"t": 441.55, "spk": "B", "text": "Question 20. An associative entity is used to implement:"}, {"t": 445.31, "spk": "B", "text": "A: A one-to-one relationship. B: A many-to-many relationship. C: A weak entity. D: A supertype."}, {"t": 455.15, "spk": "A", "text": "The answer is B: A many-to-many relationship."}, {"t": 458.46, "spk": "A", "text": "It holds the identifiers of both sides and becomes the junction table."}, {"t": 463.34, "spk": "B", "text": "Question 21. A weak entity becomes a weak table. Its primary key is:"}, {"t": 468.17, "spk": "B", "text": "A: A new auto-increment column only. B: Composite: a foreign key to the identifying table plus another column. C: Exactly the identifying table's primary key. D: It has no primary key."}, {"t": 482.45, "spk": "A", "text": "The answer is B: Composite: a foreign key to the identifying table plus another column."}, {"t": 488.02, "spk": "A", "text": "The weak table borrows the identifying table's key and adds a column to make it unique."}, {"t": 493.38, "spk": "B", "text": "Question 22. A subtype entity becomes a subtype table. Its primary key is:"}, {"t": 498.43, "spk": "B", "text": "A: A new artificial key. B: The supertype's primary key, which is also a foreign key to it. C: A composite of all its attributes. D: Any unique column."}, {"t": 511.2, "spk": "A", "text": "The answer is B: The supertype's primary key, which is also a foreign key to it."}, {"t": 516.28, "spk": "A", "text": "Subtype rows line up one-to-one with supertype rows."}, {"t": 520.4, "spk": "B", "text": "Question 23. Which is the best primary key for an Employee table?"}, {"t": 524.45, "spk": "B", "text": "A: Full name. B: Email address. C: An auto-generated EmployeeID. D: Home phone number."}, {"t": 533.53, "spk": "A", "text": "The answer is C: An auto-generated EmployeeID."}, {"t": 537.3, "spk": "A", "text": "Names, emails, and phones can change or repeat. An artificial key is stable, simple, and meaningless."}, {"t": 544.67, "spk": "B", "text": "Question 24. Why should a primary key be stable?"}, {"t": 547.71, "spk": "B", "text": "A: Stable keys use less storage. B: A changed primary key must also change in every matching foreign key. C: Stable keys sort faster. D: Unstable keys can't be indexed."}, {"t": 561.64, "spk": "A", "text": "The answer is B: A changed primary key must also change in every matching foreign key."}, {"t": 566.95, "spk": "A", "text": "Changing a key ripples through every table that references it."}, {"t": 570.95, "spk": "B", "text": "Question 25. A non-key column depends on only part of a composite primary key. Which normal form does this break?"}, {"t": 577.81, "spk": "B", "text": "A: 1NF. B: 2NF. C: Only 3NF. D: Only BCNF."}, {"t": 587.4, "spk": "A", "text": "The answer is B: 2NF."}, {"t": 590.28, "spk": "A", "text": "2NF requires every non-key column to depend on the whole primary key."}, {"t": 595.86, "spk": "B", "text": "Question 26. A table can have several candidate keys. The one the designer chooses is the:"}, {"t": 601.66, "spk": "B", "text": "A: Foreign key. B: Primary key. C: Artificial key. D: Composite key."}, {"t": 610.15, "spk": "A", "text": "The answer is B: Primary key."}, {"t": 612.97, "spk": "A", "text": "Candidate keys are the options; the primary key is the pick."}, {"t": 617.42, "spk": "B", "text": "Question 27. In Employee(ID, Name, DeptCode, DeptName), DeptName depends on DeptCode. What's the fix?"}, {"t": 625.62, "spk": "B", "text": "A: Add an index on DeptName. B: Move DeptCode and DeptName into a Department table. C: Make DeptName the primary key. D: Denormalize the table."}, {"t": 638.05, "spk": "A", "text": "The answer is B: Move DeptCode and DeptName into a Department table."}, {"t": 642.78, "spk": "A", "text": "Decompose into a higher normal form. Employee keeps DeptCode as a foreign key."}, {"t": 648.65, "spk": "B", "text": "Question 28. Every cell holds one value, and the table has a primary key. That's the minimum for:"}, {"t": 654.67, "spk": "B", "text": "A: 1NF. B: 2NF. C: 3NF. D: BCNF."}, {"t": 663.56, "spk": "A", "text": "The answer is A: 1NF."}, {"t": 666.4, "spk": "A", "text": "1NF: single values, a primary key, non-key columns depend on it."}, {"t": 672.5, "spk": "B", "text": "Question 29. Which table structure is fastest for bulk loading many rows?"}, {"t": 677.25, "spk": "B", "text": "A: Heap table. B: Sorted table. C: Hash table. D: Table cluster."}, {"t": 685.16, "spk": "A", "text": "The answer is A: Heap table."}, {"t": 687.43, "spk": "A", "text": "No order to maintain, so rows go in load order."}, {"t": 691.06, "spk": "B", "text": "Question 30. Which structure interleaves rows of two or more tables in the same storage area?"}, {"t": 696.45, "spk": "B", "text": "A: Hash table. B: Tablespace. C: Table cluster. D: Bitmap index."}, {"t": 704.69, "spk": "A", "text": "The answer is C: Table cluster."}, {"t": 707.31, "spk": "A", "text": "Table clusters speed up joins on the cluster key."}, {"t": 711.03, "spk": "B", "text": "Question 31. A query selects a high percentage of rows (high hit ratio). Which is usually faster?"}, {"t": 717.18, "spk": "B", "text": "A: Index scan. B: Table scan. C: Binary search. D: Hash index."}, {"t": 725.46, "spk": "A", "text": "The answer is B: Table scan."}, {"t": 728.12, "spk": "A", "text": "High hit ratio: table scan. Low hit ratio: index scan."}, {"t": 733.66, "spk": "B", "text": "Question 32. A sparse index contains:"}, {"t": 736.93, "spk": "B", "text": "A: An entry for every table row. B: An entry for every table block. C: Only NULL values. D: A grid of bits."}, {"t": 746.99, "spk": "A", "text": "The answer is B: An entry for every table block."}, {"t": 750.27, "spk": "A", "text": "Dense = per row; sparse = per block."}, {"t": 754.14, "spk": "B", "text": "Question 33. A tablespace is a database object that:"}, {"t": 758.37, "spk": "B", "text": "A: Maps one or more tables to a single file. B: Stores a view's results. C: Splits a table into partitions. D: Holds user permissions."}, {"t": 770.66, "spk": "A", "text": "The answer is A: Maps one or more tables to a single file."}, {"t": 774.75, "spk": "A", "text": "Created with CREATE TABLESPACE."}, {"t": 777.67, "spk": "B", "text": "Question 34. Which index type has an entry for every table row?"}, {"t": 781.88, "spk": "B", "text": "A: Sparse. B: Dense. C: Bitmap. D: Logical."}, {"t": 788.89, "spk": "A", "text": "The answer is B: Dense."}, {"t": 791.16, "spk": "A", "text": "Dense = per row. Sparse = per block."}, {"t": 794.78, "spk": "B", "text": "Question 35. Which index type suits a column with few distinct values, like Yes/No?"}, {"t": 800.5, "spk": "B", "text": "A: Bitmap index. B: Dense index. C: Hash index. D: Function index."}, {"t": 809.39, "spk": "A", "text": "The answer is A: Bitmap index."}, {"t": 811.89, "spk": "A", "text": "A bitmap index is a grid of 1s and 0s per value."}, {"t": 816.33, "spk": "B", "text": "Question 36. A hash table uses a modulo function with 10 buckets. Which bucket does key 47 go to?"}, {"t": 822.91, "spk": "B", "text": "A: 4. B: 7. C: 47. D: 0."}, {"t": 829.63, "spk": "A", "text": "The answer is B: 7."}, {"t": 831.71, "spk": "A", "text": "47 ÷ 10 leaves remainder 7."}, {"t": 835.33, "spk": "B", "text": "That's the episode. Short break, then keep going."}, {"t": 838.14, "spk": "A", "text": "You've got this."}]}, "quiz-c2": {"title": "Quiz show: Competency 2", "file": "audio/quiz-c2.mp3?v=4dd73b51", "dur": 603.35, "segs": [{"t": 0.0, "spk": "B", "text": "Welcome to the D426 study podcast. I'm Sam."}, {"t": 3.68, "spk": "A", "text": "And I'm Alex."}, {"t": 5.25, "spk": "A", "text": "Quiz show: every practice question for Competency 2, Determines SQL Queries. That's 28 questions."}, {"t": 12.51, "spk": "B", "text": "I'll read the question and the choices, you answer in your head, then Alex explains."}, {"t": 17.41, "spk": "B", "text": "Question 1. What is the correct order of clauses in a SELECT statement?"}, {"t": 21.83, "spk": "B", "text": "A: SELECT, FROM, WHERE, GROUP BY, HAVING, ORDER BY. B: SELECT, FROM, GROUP BY, WHERE, ORDER BY, HAVING. C: SELECT, WHERE, FROM, HAVING, GROUP BY, ORDER BY. D: FROM, SELECT, WHERE, ORDER BY, GROUP BY, HAVING."}, {"t": 41.98, "spk": "A", "text": "The answer is A: SELECT, FROM, WHERE, GROUP BY, HAVING, ORDER BY."}, {"t": 47.12, "spk": "A", "text": "Mnemonic: Sweaty Feet Will Give Horrible Odors."}, {"t": 50.7, "spk": "B", "text": "Question 2. What does the HAVING clause do?"}, {"t": 53.27, "spk": "B", "text": "A: Filters individual rows before grouping. B: Filters group results after GROUP BY. C: Sorts the result set. D: Joins two tables."}, {"t": 65.08, "spk": "A", "text": "The answer is B: Filters group results after GROUP BY."}, {"t": 68.91, "spk": "A", "text": "HAVING filters groups. WHERE filters rows before grouping."}, {"t": 72.93, "spk": "B", "text": "Question 3. Which value matches LIKE 'L_t'?"}, {"t": 76.35, "spk": "B", "text": "A: Lt. B: Loot. C: Lift. D: Lot."}, {"t": 83.16, "spk": "A", "text": "The answer is D: Lot."}, {"t": 85.33, "spk": "A", "text": "_ matches exactly one character."}, {"t": 88.84, "spk": "B", "text": "Question 4. value BETWEEN 10 AND 20 is equivalent to:"}, {"t": 92.54, "spk": "B", "text": "A: value > 10 AND value < 20. B: value >= 10 AND value <= 20. C: value >= 10 OR value <= 20. D: value IN (10, 20)."}, {"t": 106.32, "spk": "A", "text": "The answer is B: value >= 10 AND value <= 20."}, {"t": 110.91, "spk": "A", "text": "BETWEEN includes both endpoints."}, {"t": 113.7, "spk": "B", "text": "Question 5. What does SUBSTRING('Database', 1, 4) return?"}, {"t": 117.94, "spk": "B", "text": "A: 'base'. B: 'Data'. C: 'Datab'. D: 'atab'."}, {"t": 124.01, "spk": "A", "text": "The answer is B: 'Data'."}, {"t": 126.05, "spk": "A", "text": "Start at position 1, take 4 characters."}, {"t": 129.53, "spk": "B", "text": "Question 6. Which returns the number of rows in a table, even when some columns are NULL?"}, {"t": 134.91, "spk": "B", "text": "A: COUNT(*). B: SUM(*). C: COUNT(NULL). D: MAX(*)."}, {"t": 142.6, "spk": "A", "text": "The answer is A: COUNT(*)."}, {"t": 145.19, "spk": "A", "text": "COUNT(*) counts rows. The other aggregates skip NULL values."}, {"t": 149.88, "spk": "B", "text": "Question 7. How do you sort results from highest to lowest salary?"}, {"t": 153.65, "spk": "B", "text": "A: ORDER BY Salary. B: ORDER BY Salary DESC. C: GROUP BY Salary DESC. D: SORT BY Salary DOWN."}, {"t": 163.45, "spk": "A", "text": "The answer is B: ORDER BY Salary DESC."}, {"t": 166.39, "spk": "A", "text": "ORDER BY is ascending unless you add DESC."}, {"t": 169.71, "spk": "B", "text": "Question 8. State IN ('PA', 'NJ') is the same as:"}, {"t": 173.17, "spk": "B", "text": "A: State = 'PA' AND State = 'NJ'. B: State = 'PA' OR State = 'NJ'. C: State BETWEEN 'PA' AND 'NJ'. D: State LIKE 'PA%NJ'."}, {"t": 187.16, "spk": "A", "text": "The answer is B: State = 'PA' OR State = 'NJ'."}, {"t": 191.59, "spk": "A", "text": "IN matches any value in the list."}, {"t": 194.56, "spk": "B", "text": "Question 9. Which condition finds rows where Phone has no value?"}, {"t": 198.68, "spk": "B", "text": "A: Phone = NULL. B: Phone IS NULL. C: Phone = ''. D: Phone LIKE NULL."}, {"t": 207.26, "spk": "A", "text": "The answer is B: Phone IS NULL."}, {"t": 209.65, "spk": "A", "text": "= NULL never matches. Use IS NULL."}, {"t": 213.18, "spk": "B", "text": "Question 10. Aggregate functions in a SELECT process which rows?"}, {"t": 217.36, "spk": "B", "text": "A: Every row in the table, always. B: Only rows that satisfy the WHERE clause. C: Only rows in the first group. D: Only rows with no NULLs."}, {"t": 229.32, "spk": "A", "text": "The answer is B: Only rows that satisfy the WHERE clause."}, {"t": 233.26, "spk": "A", "text": "No WHERE clause means every row."}, {"t": 236.33, "spk": "B", "text": "Question 11. Which keyword gives a column a temporary name in the result?"}, {"t": 240.68, "spk": "B", "text": "A: AS. B: RENAME. C: ALIAS. D: NAME."}, {"t": 246.48, "spk": "A", "text": "The answer is A: AS."}, {"t": 248.43, "spk": "A", "text": "Salary AS Pay. AS is optional but common."}, {"t": 251.47, "spk": "B", "text": "Question 12. What does CONCAT('Data', 'base') return?"}, {"t": 254.83, "spk": "B", "text": "A: 'Data base'. B: 'Database'. C: 'Data'. D: An error."}, {"t": 262.02, "spk": "A", "text": "The answer is B: 'Database'."}, {"t": 264.29, "spk": "A", "text": "CONCAT joins strings end to end, no space added."}, {"t": 268.25, "spk": "B", "text": "Question 13. Which ending completes 'SELECT DeptCode FROM Employee …' to list departments with more than 5 employees?"}, {"t": 275.15, "spk": "B", "text": "A: WHERE COUNT(*) > 5. B: GROUP BY DeptCode HAVING COUNT(*) > 5. C: ORDER BY COUNT(*) > 5. D: HAVING COUNT(*) > 5 GROUP BY DeptCode."}, {"t": 289.98, "spk": "A", "text": "The answer is B: GROUP BY DeptCode HAVING COUNT(*) > 5."}, {"t": 295.05, "spk": "A", "text": "Filter on an aggregate with HAVING, and HAVING comes after GROUP BY."}, {"t": 299.5, "spk": "B", "text": "Question 14. A LEFT JOIN returns:"}, {"t": 302.36, "spk": "B", "text": "A: Only matching rows from both tables. B: All right rows and matching left rows. C: All left rows and only matching right rows. D: Every combination of rows."}, {"t": 315.2, "spk": "A", "text": "The answer is C: All left rows and only matching right rows."}, {"t": 319.18, "spk": "A", "text": "Unmatched left rows get NULLs in the right table's columns."}, {"t": 323.32, "spk": "B", "text": "Question 15. In a FULL JOIN, how do unmatched left table rows appear?"}, {"t": 327.8, "spk": "B", "text": "A: They are dropped. B: With NULLs in the right table's columns. C: Duplicated. D: With zeros in the right table's columns."}, {"t": 338.3, "spk": "A", "text": "The answer is B: With NULLs in the right table's columns."}, {"t": 341.66, "spk": "A", "text": "FULL JOIN keeps everything from both sides and fills gaps with NULL."}, {"t": 346.27, "spk": "B", "text": "Question 16. Which join compares no columns and has no ON clause?"}, {"t": 350.71, "spk": "B", "text": "A: Equijoin. B: Self-join. C: INNER JOIN. D: CROSS JOIN."}, {"t": 358.45, "spk": "A", "text": "The answer is D: CROSS JOIN."}, {"t": 361.13, "spk": "A", "text": "A cross-join returns every combination of rows."}, {"t": 364.86, "spk": "B", "text": "Question 17. Joining Employee to itself to match employees with managers is a:"}, {"t": 369.92, "spk": "B", "text": "A: Self-join. B: Cross-join. C: Non-equijoin. D: Subquery."}, {"t": 377.87, "spk": "A", "text": "The answer is A: Self-join."}, {"t": 380.07, "spk": "A", "text": "Use aliases to tell the two copies apart."}, {"t": 383.39, "spk": "B", "text": "Question 18. An equijoin compares columns using which operator?"}, {"t": 387.83, "spk": "B", "text": "A: <. B: =. C: >. D: LIKE."}, {"t": 393.79, "spk": "A", "text": "The answer is B: =."}, {"t": 396.01, "spk": "A", "text": "Most joins are equijoins. Non-equijoins use < or > etc."}, {"t": 401.64, "spk": "B", "text": "Question 19. What is a materialized view?"}, {"t": 404.36, "spk": "B", "text": "A: A view whose data is stored and must be refreshed when base tables change. B: A view that can't be queried. C: A base table. D: A view with WITH CHECK OPTION."}, {"t": 416.36, "spk": "A", "text": "The answer is A: A view whose data is stored and must be refreshed when base tables change."}, {"t": 422.12, "spk": "A", "text": "Normal views run their query on use; materialized views store results."}, {"t": 427.26, "spk": "B", "text": "Question 20. What does WITH CHECK OPTION do on a view?"}, {"t": 430.06, "spk": "B", "text": "A: Speeds up the view. B: Rejects inserts/updates through the view that fail its WHERE clause. C: Checks the view for syntax errors. D: Refreshes a materialized view."}, {"t": 443.44, "spk": "A", "text": "The answer is B: Rejects inserts/updates through the view that fail its WHERE clause."}, {"t": 448.76, "spk": "A", "text": "The database returns an error explaining the violation."}, {"t": 452.48, "spk": "B", "text": "Question 21. A subquery is also called a:"}, {"t": 455.95, "spk": "B", "text": "A: Nested query. B: View. C: Cross-join. D: Union."}, {"t": 463.6, "spk": "A", "text": "The answer is A: Nested query."}, {"t": 465.87, "spk": "A", "text": "Also called an inner query."}, {"t": 468.58, "spk": "B", "text": "Question 22. Which keyword stacks the results of two SELECT statements into one result?"}, {"t": 474.03, "spk": "B", "text": "A: JOIN. B: UNION. C: MERGE. D: GROUP BY."}, {"t": 480.87, "spk": "A", "text": "The answer is B: UNION."}, {"t": 482.88, "spk": "A", "text": "UNION combines two results into one table."}, {"t": 486.41, "spk": "B", "text": "Question 23. The tables a view's SELECT reads from are called:"}, {"t": 490.3, "spk": "B", "text": "A: Base tables. B: Temp tables. C: Materialized tables. D: Child tables."}, {"t": 499.01, "spk": "A", "text": "The answer is A: Base tables."}, {"t": 501.44, "spk": "A", "text": "A view is defined by a query over its base tables."}, {"t": 505.35, "spk": "B", "text": "Question 24. Which join compares columns with an operator like < or >?"}, {"t": 509.74, "spk": "B", "text": "A: Equijoin. B: Non-equijoin. C: Self-join. D: Natural join."}, {"t": 517.91, "spk": "A", "text": "The answer is B: Non-equijoin."}, {"t": 520.5, "spk": "A", "text": "Equijoins use =. Anything else is a non-equijoin."}, {"t": 525.32, "spk": "B", "text": "Question 25. Which joins are outer joins?"}, {"t": 528.33, "spk": "B", "text": "A: INNER only. B: LEFT, RIGHT, and FULL. C: CROSS and INNER. D: Self-joins only."}, {"t": 537.54, "spk": "A", "text": "The answer is B: LEFT, RIGHT, and FULL."}, {"t": 541.02, "spk": "A", "text": "Outer joins keep unmatched rows."}, {"t": 544.07, "spk": "B", "text": "Question 26. In a self-join, what must you give each copy of the table?"}, {"t": 548.45, "spk": "B", "text": "A: A primary key. B: An alias. C: An index. D: A view."}, {"t": 556.13, "spk": "A", "text": "The answer is B: An alias."}, {"t": 558.61, "spk": "A", "text": "Aliases tell the two copies apart."}, {"t": 561.62, "spk": "B", "text": "Question 27. An INNER JOIN returns:"}, {"t": 564.33, "spk": "B", "text": "A: All rows from both tables. B: Only rows that match in both tables. C: All left table rows. D: Every combination of rows."}, {"t": 575.49, "spk": "A", "text": "The answer is B: Only rows that match in both tables."}, {"t": 578.88, "spk": "A", "text": "Unmatched rows are dropped."}, {"t": 581.41, "spk": "B", "text": "Question 28. Employee has 10 rows and Department has 4. How many rows does a CROSS JOIN return?"}, {"t": 587.22, "spk": "B", "text": "A: 14. B: 10. C: 40. D: 4."}, {"t": 593.45, "spk": "A", "text": "The answer is C: 40."}, {"t": 595.4, "spk": "A", "text": "Every combination: 10 × 4 = 40."}, {"t": 599.24, "spk": "B", "text": "That's the episode. Short break, then keep going."}, {"t": 602.0, "spk": "A", "text": "You've got this."}]}, "quiz-c1": {"title": "Quiz show: Competency 1", "file": "audio/quiz-c1.mp3?v=06195ffe", "dur": 451.71, "segs": [{"t": 0.0, "spk": "B", "text": "Welcome to the D426 study podcast. I'm Sam."}, {"t": 3.71, "spk": "A", "text": "And I'm Alex."}, {"t": 5.35, "spk": "A", "text": "Quiz show: every practice question for Competency 1, Explains Database Attributes and SQL Commands. That's 19 questions."}, {"t": 14.2, "spk": "B", "text": "I'll read the question and the choices, you answer in your head, then Alex explains."}, {"t": 19.16, "spk": "B", "text": "Question 1. Which component translates query processor instructions into low-level file-system commands?"}, {"t": 25.26, "spk": "B", "text": "A: Transaction manager. B: Query optimizer. C: Storage manager. D: Catalog."}, {"t": 34.06, "spk": "A", "text": "The answer is C: Storage manager."}, {"t": 36.71, "spk": "A", "text": "The storage manager does low-level reads and writes and uses indexes."}, {"t": 41.37, "spk": "B", "text": "Question 2. Which component restores the database to a consistent state after a system failure?"}, {"t": 46.53, "spk": "B", "text": "A: Transaction manager. B: Storage manager. C: Query processor. D: Database administrator."}, {"t": 55.97, "spk": "A", "text": "The answer is A: Transaction manager."}, {"t": 58.72, "spk": "A", "text": "The transaction manager also prevents conflicts between concurrent transactions."}, {"t": 64.36, "spk": "B", "text": "Question 3. Which component performs query optimization?"}, {"t": 68.12, "spk": "B", "text": "A: Storage manager. B: Query processor. C: Transaction manager. D: Tablespace."}, {"t": 76.92, "spk": "A", "text": "The answer is B: Query processor."}, {"t": 79.79, "spk": "A", "text": "The query processor interprets, plans, optimizes, and returns results."}, {"t": 85.28, "spk": "B", "text": "Question 4. Who secures the database system against unauthorized users?"}, {"t": 89.71, "spk": "B", "text": "A: Database programmer. B: Database user. C: Database designer. D: Database administrator."}, {"t": 99.06, "spk": "A", "text": "The answer is D: Database administrator."}, {"t": 102.04, "spk": "A", "text": "The DBA also enforces procedures for user access and availability."}, {"t": 107.21, "spk": "B", "text": "Question 5. In relational terminology, a column is also called a(n):"}, {"t": 112.08, "spk": "B", "text": "A: Tuple. B: Relation. C: Attribute. D: Record."}, {"t": 119.07, "spk": "A", "text": "The answer is C: Attribute."}, {"t": 121.47, "spk": "A", "text": "Table = Relation = File; Row = Tuple = Record; Column = Attribute = Field."}, {"t": 129.36, "spk": "B", "text": "Question 6. Why do the rows of a table have no inherent order?"}, {"t": 132.98, "spk": "B", "text": "A: Because a table is a set of rows. B: Because rows are tuples. C: Because of the primary key. D: Because tables are stored as heaps."}, {"t": 145.45, "spk": "A", "text": "The answer is A: Because a table is a set of rows."}, {"t": 148.89, "spk": "A", "text": "Sets are unordered. Tuples themselves ARE ordered."}, {"t": 152.59, "spk": "B", "text": "Question 7. The principle that physical design never affects query results is called:"}, {"t": 157.75, "spk": "B", "text": "A: Referential integrity. B: Data independence. C: Normalization. D: Functional dependence."}, {"t": 167.36, "spk": "A", "text": "The answer is B: Data independence."}, {"t": 169.98, "spk": "A", "text": "Data independence lets DBAs reorganize storage without changing results."}, {"t": 175.36, "spk": "B", "text": "Question 8. What is the minimum a table must have?"}, {"t": 178.21, "spk": "B", "text": "A: One row and one column. B: A primary key and one row. C: At least one column; any number of rows. D: Two columns."}, {"t": 189.4, "spk": "A", "text": "The answer is C: At least one column; any number of rows."}, {"t": 193.71, "spk": "A", "text": "A table with no rows is valid; it's called an empty table."}, {"t": 198.01, "spk": "B", "text": "Question 9. Which relational algebra operation selects columns?"}, {"t": 202.27, "spk": "B", "text": "A: Select. B: Project. C: Product. D: Union."}, {"t": 208.87, "spk": "A", "text": "The answer is B: Project."}, {"t": 211.16, "spk": "A", "text": "Project picks columns; select picks rows; product pairs every row."}, {"t": 216.84, "spk": "B", "text": "Question 10. Which SQL sublanguage includes CREATE, ALTER, and DROP?"}, {"t": 221.82, "spk": "B", "text": "A: DML. B: DDL. C: DQL. D: DCL."}, {"t": 229.72, "spk": "A", "text": "The answer is B: DDL."}, {"t": 232.23, "spk": "A", "text": "Data Definition Language defines structure. DML (INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE) changes the data itself."}, {"t": 239.29, "spk": "B", "text": "Question 11. Data Control Language (DCL) is used to:"}, {"t": 243.22, "spk": "B", "text": "A: Retrieve data. B: Manage transactions. C: Control database user access. D: Define table structure."}, {"t": 253.21, "spk": "A", "text": "The answer is C: Control database user access."}, {"t": 256.63, "spk": "A", "text": "DCL controls access (GRANT, REVOKE). DTL manages transactions; DQL retrieves; DDL defines structure."}, {"t": 265.92, "spk": "B", "text": "Question 12. DECIMAL(5, 2) stores:"}, {"t": 268.7, "spk": "B", "text": "A: 5 digits before and 2 after the decimal. B: 5 total digits, 2 after the decimal. C: Values between 5 and 2. D: 5 characters of text."}, {"t": 281.84, "spk": "A", "text": "The answer is B: 5 total digits, 2 after the decimal."}, {"t": 285.69, "spk": "A", "text": "DECIMAL(M, D): M total digits, D after the decimal. Max 999.99."}, {"t": 293.31, "spk": "B", "text": "Question 13. How many bytes does SMALLINT use?"}, {"t": 296.38, "spk": "B", "text": "A: 1. B: 2. C: 4. D: 8."}, {"t": 302.53, "spk": "A", "text": "The answer is B: 2."}, {"t": 304.8, "spk": "A", "text": "TINYINT 1, SMALLINT 2, MEDIUMINT 3, INT 4, BIGINT 8."}, {"t": 309.97, "spk": "B", "text": "Question 14. In the five-sublanguage model, SELECT belongs to:"}, {"t": 314.26, "spk": "B", "text": "A: DML. B: DDL. C: DQL. D: DCL."}, {"t": 322.15, "spk": "A", "text": "The answer is C: DQL."}, {"t": 324.56, "spk": "A", "text": "Some sheets lump SELECT with DML; the five-sublanguage model puts it in DQL."}, {"t": 330.45, "spk": "B", "text": "Question 15. What happens if you run UPDATE with no WHERE clause?"}, {"t": 334.06, "spk": "B", "text": "A: Nothing is updated. B: Only the first row is updated. C: An error is returned. D: Every row in the table is updated."}, {"t": 344.5, "spk": "A", "text": "The answer is D: Every row in the table is updated."}, {"t": 348.24, "spk": "A", "text": "Omitting WHERE in UPDATE or DELETE affects all rows."}, {"t": 351.92, "spk": "B", "text": "Question 16. How does DROP TABLE differ from TRUNCATE?"}, {"t": 355.24, "spk": "B", "text": "A: DROP deletes the table and its rows; TRUNCATE deletes all rows but keeps the table. B: DROP deletes some rows; TRUNCATE deletes the table. C: They are identical. D: TRUNCATE requires a WHERE clause."}, {"t": 370.42, "spk": "A", "text": "The answer is A: DROP deletes the table and its rows; TRUNCATE deletes all rows but keeps the table."}, {"t": 376.3, "spk": "A", "text": "TRUNCATE is nearly identical to DELETE with no WHERE. DROP removes the structure too."}, {"t": 381.84, "spk": "B", "text": "Question 17. Which is true of a foreign key and the primary key it references?"}, {"t": 386.4, "spk": "B", "text": "A: Names and data types must both match. B: Data types must match; names may differ. C: Names must match; data types may differ. D: Neither needs to match."}, {"t": 399.84, "spk": "A", "text": "The answer is B: Data types must match; names may differ."}, {"t": 404.22, "spk": "A", "text": "Same data type required; names can differ."}, {"t": 407.56, "spk": "B", "text": "Question 18. Which action propagates primary key changes to foreign keys?"}, {"t": 412.3, "spk": "B", "text": "A: RESTRICT. B: SET NULL. C: CASCADE. D: SET DEFAULT."}, {"t": 419.55, "spk": "A", "text": "The answer is C: CASCADE."}, {"t": 421.94, "spk": "A", "text": "CASCADE propagates. RESTRICT rejects. SET NULL / SET DEFAULT replace the invalid foreign key."}, {"t": 428.27, "spk": "B", "text": "Question 19. Which action rejects an insert, update, or delete that violates referential integrity?"}, {"t": 434.58, "spk": "B", "text": "A: RESTRICT. B: CASCADE. C: SET NULL. D: SET DEFAULT."}, {"t": 441.83, "spk": "A", "text": "The answer is A: RESTRICT."}, {"t": 444.11, "spk": "A", "text": "RESTRICT simply refuses the statement."}, {"t": 447.41, "spk": "B", "text": "That's the episode. Short break, then keep going."}, {"t": 450.25, "spk": "A", "text": "You've got this."}]}}};
